export const igcsePhysicsNotes: Record<string, string> = {
  "igcse-u1": "# Unit 1 — Physical quantities and measurement\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 1.1, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Measuring length, volume and time — 1.1\n\nEvery measurement is a number and a unit, such as 25.0 cm. A ruler measures length; read it with your eye level with the mark to avoid parallax error, and measure from the zero mark (or subtract the starting reading if the zero is worn). A measuring cylinder measures the volume of a liquid; read the bottom of the meniscus at eye level, with the cylinder on a flat surface. 1 cm³ = 1 ml, and 1000 cm³ = 1 litre.\n\nClocks and stopwatches measure time intervals. A hand-operated stopwatch includes your reaction time (about 0.2 to 0.3 s) at both the start and the stop, so it is unreliable for short intervals. Digital timers started and stopped by light gates remove reaction time and suit short, fast events.\n\n### Measuring multiples to reduce uncertainty — 1.1\n\nSmall lengths and short times are hard to measure directly, so measure many together and divide. Measure the thickness of 100 sheets of paper and divide by 100. Time 20 oscillations of a pendulum and divide by 20 to get the period. The reading uncertainty (and reaction time) is spread over all the repeats, so the average for one is far more precise. Repeat the whole measurement and take the mean to reduce the effect of random errors.\n\nOne oscillation of a pendulum is a complete swing: from one side, to the other, and back to the start. Start timing as the bob passes the centre (it moves fastest there, so the moment is easy to judge), and count \"zero\" as you start the watch.\n\n### Scalars and vectors — 1.1 (Supplement)\n\nA scalar has magnitude (size) only. A vector has magnitude and direction.\n\n- Scalars: distance, speed, time, mass, energy, temperature.\n- Vectors: force, weight, velocity, acceleration, momentum, electric field strength, gravitational field strength.\n\nSpeed and velocity show the difference: 20 m/s is a speed; 20 m/s due north is a velocity. Two cars moving at 20 m/s in opposite directions have the same speed but different velocities.\n\n### Adding two vectors at right angles — 1.1 (Supplement)\n\nThe resultant is the single vector that has the same effect as the two together. For two vectors at 90° to each other (forces or velocities only in this syllabus), draw them tip-to-tail: the resultant runs from the tail of the first to the tip of the second, closing a right-angled triangle.\n\nBy calculation, Pythagoras gives the size and tan gives the angle. By scale drawing, choose a scale (for example 1 cm = 1 N), draw the two vectors at right angles, draw the resultant, measure its length and convert back, and measure the angle with a protractor. Always state the direction of the resultant as well as its size.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| value of one = total measurement ÷ number measured | For example: period T = total time ÷ number of oscillations | Small distances and short times measured as multiples. | Core |\n| mean = sum of readings ÷ number of readings | Same unit as the readings | Repeated readings of the same quantity. Leave out an obvious anomaly. | Core |\n| resultant R = √(X² + Y²) | X, Y: the two perpendicular vectors (N for forces, m/s for velocities); R: same unit | Two vectors at exactly 90°. | Supplement |\n| tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent = Y ÷ X | θ: angle of the resultant measured from X, in degrees | Gives the direction of the resultant. | Supplement |\n\nUseful conversions: 1 m = 100 cm = 1000 mm; 1 km = 1000 m; 1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³; 1 cm³ = 1 ml; 1 kg = 1000 g; 1 minute = 60 s; 1 hour = 3600 s.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: a stack or a coil of identical things\n\n**Example — thickness of one sheet.** A stack of 250 sheets of paper is 2.5 cm thick.\n\n- Thickness of one sheet = 2.5 cm ÷ 250 = 0.010 cm = 0.10 mm.\n- The same method gives the diameter of a thin wire: wind 30 tight turns around a pencil, measure the width of the coil, divide by 30.\n\n### Situation: timing a pendulum\n\n**Example — period.** A student times 20 complete oscillations and gets 31.2 s, 30.8 s and 31.0 s in three trials.\n\n- Mean time for 20 oscillations = (31.2 + 30.8 + 31.0) ÷ 3 = 31.0 s.\n- Period T = 31.0 s ÷ 20 = 1.55 s.\n- Why not time one swing? A reaction time of about 0.2 s would be a large fraction of 1.55 s; over 31 s it hardly matters.\n\n### Situation: volume by measuring cylinder\n\n**Example — volume of liquid poured out.** A measuring cylinder reads 74 cm³ before and 46 cm³ after some liquid is poured out.\n\n- Volume poured = 74 − 46 = 28 cm³.\n\n### Situation: two forces at right angles (Supplement)\n\n**Example — resultant force.** A boat is pulled by a 30 N force east and a 40 N force north.\n\n- R = √(30² + 40²) = √(900 + 1600) = √2500 = 50 N.\n- tan θ = 40 ÷ 30 = 1.33, so θ = 53° north of east.\n- Answer: 50 N at 53° north of east (a bearing of 037°).\n\n### Situation: two velocities at right angles (Supplement)\n\n**Example — swimmer crossing a river.** A swimmer swims at 1.2 m/s straight across a river that flows at 0.5 m/s.\n\n- Resultant velocity = √(1.2² + 0.5²) = √(1.44 + 0.25) = √1.69 = 1.3 m/s.\n- tan θ = 0.5 ÷ 1.2 = 0.417, so θ = 23° downstream from the straight-across direction.\n\n### Situation: scale drawing (Supplement)\n\n**Example — drawing the resultant.** Forces of 6.0 N and 8.0 N act at right angles. Using 1 cm = 1 N, draw 6.0 cm and 8.0 cm lines at 90°, tip-to-tail. The closing side measures 10.0 cm, so the resultant is 10 N. Measure the angle at the start of the 6.0 N line with a protractor: about 53°.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Give a unit with every measured or calculated value.\n- When measuring multiples, say what you measured, how many, and that you divided by that number.\n- Name the error you are avoiding: parallax (eye level), zero error (start from zero), reaction time (use multiples or light gates).\n- A vector answer needs a size and a direction.\n- Pythagoras only works when the two vectors are at 90°. Vectors along one line are simply added or subtracted (see Unit 4).\n",
  "igcse-u2": "# Unit 2 — Motion\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 1.2, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Speed and velocity — 1.2\n\nSpeed is the distance travelled per unit time. Velocity is speed in a given direction, so velocity is a vector and speed is a scalar. A car going round a roundabout at a steady 10 m/s has a constant speed but a changing velocity, because its direction changes.\n\nMost journeys do not happen at a steady speed, so the useful quantity is the average speed: the total distance divided by the total time, including any time spent stopped.\n\n### Distance–time graphs — 1.2\n\nThe gradient (steepness) of a distance–time graph is the speed.\n\n- Horizontal line: at rest (distance not changing).\n- Straight sloping line: constant speed. A steeper line means a faster speed.\n- Curve getting steeper: accelerating.\n- Curve getting less steep: decelerating.\n\nTo find a speed, pick two points far apart on a straight section, then divide the change in distance by the change in time.\n\n### Speed–time graphs — 1.2\n\nThe gradient of a speed–time graph is the acceleration, and the area under it is the distance travelled.\n\n- Horizontal line: constant speed (zero acceleration). On the time axis: at rest.\n- Straight line sloping up: constant acceleration. Sloping down: constant deceleration.\n- Curve (Supplement): changing acceleration. A curve that gets less steep shows acceleration decreasing, for example as air resistance grows.\n\nFor the distance, split the area into rectangles (constant speed) and triangles (constant acceleration from or to rest), or use trapezium areas, and add them up.\n\n### Acceleration — 1.2 (Supplement)\n\nAcceleration is the change in velocity per unit time. It is measured in m/s², meaning metres per second, every second. An acceleration of 3 m/s² means the velocity rises by 3 m/s each second.\n\nA deceleration is a negative acceleration. If a car slows from 20 m/s to 8 m/s in 4 s, a = (8 − 20) ÷ 4 = −3 m/s², which is a deceleration of 3 m/s². Keep the minus sign in calculations and say \"deceleration of 3 m/s²\" in words.\n\n### Free fall and terminal velocity — 1.2\n\nNear the Earth's surface the acceleration of free fall, g, is approximately constant at about 9.8 m/s². Without air resistance, every object falls with this acceleration whatever its mass.\n\n**Supplement:** with air (or liquid) resistance, a falling object behaves in three stages.\n\n- At the start the speed is low, so air resistance is small; the resultant force is nearly the full weight and the object accelerates at about g.\n- As the speed rises, air resistance increases, the resultant force (weight − air resistance) decreases, and the acceleration decreases. The speed–time graph curves and becomes less steep.\n- When air resistance equals the weight, the resultant force is zero, the acceleration is zero and the object falls at a constant speed: its terminal velocity.\n\nWhen a skydiver opens a parachute, air resistance suddenly becomes greater than the weight, so the resultant force is upward and the skydiver decelerates. As the speed falls, the drag falls, until it equals the weight again at a new, lower terminal velocity.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| v = s ÷ t | v: speed, m/s; s: distance, m; t: time, s | Constant speed, or a single straight-line section of a distance–time graph. Rearranged: s = vt, t = s ÷ v. | Core |\n| average speed = total distance ÷ total time | m/s | A whole journey with several stages, including stops. Never average the speeds of the stages. | Core |\n| speed = gradient of distance–time graph | gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time | Reading speed from a graph. | Core |\n| distance = area under speed–time graph | rectangle: base × height; triangle: ½ × base × height | Constant speed or constant acceleration sections. | Core |\n| a = Δv ÷ Δt | a: acceleration, m/s²; Δv: change in velocity = final − initial, m/s; Δt: time taken, s | Any change in velocity. A negative answer is a deceleration. Rearranged: Δv = aΔt. | Supplement |\n| acceleration = gradient of speed–time graph | gradient = change in speed ÷ change in time | Reading acceleration from a graph. | Supplement |\n\nUnit conversion: to change km/h into m/s, divide by 3.6 (for example 72 km/h = 20 m/s). To change m/s into km/h, multiply by 3.6.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: constant speed, find the missing quantity\n\n**Example — time for a journey.** A train travels 45 km at a constant 25 m/s. How long does it take?\n\n- Convert first: 45 km = 45 000 m.\n- t = s ÷ v = 45 000 ÷ 25 = 1800 s = 30 minutes.\n\n### Situation: a journey in stages\n\n**Example — average speed.** A cyclist rides 6 km in 20 minutes, stops for 10 minutes, then rides 3 km in 15 minutes.\n\n- Total distance = 6 + 3 = 9 km = 9000 m.\n- Total time = 20 + 10 + 15 = 45 minutes = 2700 s.\n- Average speed = 9000 ÷ 2700 = 3.3 m/s. The stop counts, because it is part of the total time.\n\n### Situation: reading a distance–time graph\n\n**Example — speed from the gradient.** A straight line on a distance–time graph goes from (2 s, 10 m) to (8 s, 40 m).\n\n- Speed = (40 − 10) ÷ (8 − 2) = 30 ÷ 6 = 5.0 m/s.\n\n### Situation: distance from a speed–time graph\n\n**Example — area under the graph.** A car accelerates steadily from rest to 12 m/s in 6 s, then travels at 12 m/s for 10 s.\n\n- Stage 1 (triangle): ½ × 6 × 12 = 36 m.\n- Stage 2 (rectangle): 10 × 12 = 120 m.\n- Total distance = 156 m.\n\n**Example — trapezium.** A car slows steadily from 20 m/s to 10 m/s in 5 s. The area is a trapezium: ½ × (20 + 10) × 5 = 75 m. (You get the same answer by adding a 10 × 5 rectangle and a ½ × 5 × 10 triangle.)\n\n### Situation: acceleration from changes in velocity (Supplement)\n\n**Example — speeding up.** A bus goes from 4 m/s to 16 m/s in 8 s.\n\n- a = (16 − 4) ÷ 8 = 1.5 m/s².\n\n**Example — braking.** A car travelling at 30 m/s brakes and stops in 5 s.\n\n- a = (0 − 30) ÷ 5 = −6 m/s², a deceleration of 6 m/s².\n\n**Example — find the final speed.** A ball is dropped from rest and falls freely for 2.0 s (g = 9.8 m/s², no air resistance).\n\n- Δv = aΔt = 9.8 × 2.0 = 19.6 m/s, so its speed is 19.6 m/s (from rest).\n\n**Example — find the time.** A runner accelerates at 2.5 m/s² from 3 m/s to 8 m/s.\n\n- Δt = Δv ÷ a = (8 − 3) ÷ 2.5 = 2.0 s.\n\n### Situation: acceleration from a speed–time graph (Supplement)\n\n**Example — gradient.** A straight line on a speed–time graph rises from 5 m/s at 0 s to 25 m/s at 4 s.\n\n- a = (25 − 5) ÷ (4 − 0) = 5.0 m/s².\n- If the line is curved, the acceleration is changing: a steeper part means a larger acceleration.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Check the units before substituting: km into m, minutes or hours into s.\n- Distance–time graph: gradient = speed. Speed–time graph: gradient = acceleration, area = distance. Do not mix them up.\n- Average speed uses total distance and total time, including stops.\n- Δv is final minus initial. A negative acceleration is a deceleration.\n- A curved speed–time line means a changing acceleration (Supplement); explain terminal velocity with forces: air resistance grows until it equals the weight, so the resultant force and acceleration become zero.\n",
  "igcse-u3": "# Unit 3 — Mass, weight and density\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 1.3 and 1.4, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Mass and weight — 1.3\n\nMass is a measure of the quantity of matter in an object at rest relative to the observer. It is measured in kilograms and does not change when the object moves to the Moon.\n\nWeight is a gravitational force on an object that has mass. It is a force, so it is measured in newtons and has a direction (towards the centre of the planet). Weight depends on where you are: an astronaut of mass 70 kg weighs about 690 N on Earth but only about 110 N on the Moon, where g is about 1.6 N/kg.\n\nGravitational field strength g is the force per unit mass. On Earth it is about 9.8 N/kg. The number is the same as the acceleration of free fall in m/s², because N/kg and m/s² are equivalent units.\n\nA balance compares weights. Since the two objects are in the same gravitational field, equal weights also means equal masses, so a balance compares masses too.\n\n**Supplement:** weight is the effect of a gravitational field on a mass. Wherever there is a gravitational field, a mass in it has weight; change the field strength and the weight changes while the mass stays the same.\n\n### Density — 1.4\n\nDensity is mass per unit volume. It tells you how tightly packed the matter in a material is. Water has a density of 1000 kg/m³, which is 1.0 g/cm³. A material does not change its density if you cut it up: a small piece of steel has the same density as a large block.\n\n### Measuring density — 1.4\n\nFind the mass on a balance and the volume by one of these methods, then divide.\n\n- Regular solid (cuboid, cylinder, sphere): measure its dimensions with a ruler or calipers and calculate the volume. Cuboid: length × width × height. Cylinder: π r² × length.\n- Irregular solid that sinks: displacement. Part-fill a measuring cylinder with water, read the volume, lower the object in on a thread, read again. The volume of the object is the rise in the reading. (A displacement can with a spout into a measuring cylinder works for larger objects.)\n- Liquid: weigh an empty measuring cylinder, pour in a known volume of liquid, weigh again. The mass of liquid is the difference.\n\n### Floating and sinking — 1.4\n\nAn object floats in a liquid if its density is less than the liquid's density, and sinks if its density is greater. Ice (about 920 kg/m³) floats on water (1000 kg/m³); steel (about 7800 kg/m³) sinks.\n\n**Supplement:** two liquids that do not mix form layers, with the less dense liquid floating on top. Oil (about 900 kg/m³) floats on water.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| g = W ÷ m | g: gravitational field strength, N/kg; W: weight, N; m: mass, kg | Field strength, or weight from mass: W = mg. Mass from weight: m = W ÷ g. | Core |\n| ρ = m ÷ V | ρ (rho): density, kg/m³ or g/cm³; m: mass, kg or g; V: volume, m³ or cm³ | Any density question. Rearranged: m = ρV, V = m ÷ ρ. Keep the units matched (g with cm³, kg with m³). | Core |\n| V (cuboid) = l × w × h | lengths in m or cm | Regular solid. | Core |\n| V (displaced) = reading after − reading before | cm³ (= ml) | Irregular solid that sinks. | Core |\n\nUnit conversion: 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³. To turn g/cm³ into kg/m³, multiply by 1000.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: weight from mass, and the other way round\n\n**Example — weight on Earth.** A bag of mass 12 kg. W = mg = 12 × 9.8 = 118 N (to 3 s.f.).\n\n**Example — mass from a weight.** A newton meter reads 4.9 N. m = W ÷ g = 4.9 ÷ 9.8 = 0.50 kg.\n\n### Situation: a different planet\n\n**Example — Moon.** A rover of mass 180 kg is taken to the Moon, where g = 1.6 N/kg.\n\n- Its mass is still 180 kg.\n- Its weight on the Moon = 180 × 1.6 = 288 N, compared with 180 × 9.8 = 1764 N on Earth.\n\n**Example — finding g.** A 5.0 kg mass weighs 18.5 N on Mars. g = 18.5 ÷ 5.0 = 3.7 N/kg.\n\n### Situation: density of a regular solid\n\n**Example — metal block.** A block measures 2.0 cm × 3.0 cm × 5.0 cm and has a mass of 81 g.\n\n- V = 2.0 × 3.0 × 5.0 = 30 cm³.\n- ρ = 81 ÷ 30 = 2.7 g/cm³ = 2700 kg/m³ (aluminium).\n\n### Situation: density of an irregular solid (displacement)\n\n**Example — a stone.** Water in a measuring cylinder reads 40 cm³; with the stone in it reads 65 cm³. The stone's mass is 70 g.\n\n- V = 65 − 40 = 25 cm³.\n- ρ = 70 ÷ 25 = 2.8 g/cm³.\n\n### Situation: density of a liquid\n\n**Example — cooking oil.** Empty cylinder 120 g; with 50 cm³ of oil 166 g.\n\n- Mass of oil = 166 − 120 = 46 g.\n- ρ = 46 ÷ 50 = 0.92 g/cm³ = 920 kg/m³.\n\n### Situation: mass or volume from density\n\n**Example — mass of air in a room.** A room is 5.0 m × 4.0 m × 2.5 m; air has density 1.2 kg/m³.\n\n- V = 5.0 × 4.0 × 2.5 = 50 m³.\n- m = ρV = 1.2 × 50 = 60 kg.\n\n**Example — volume of a gold bar.** A gold bar has mass 12.5 kg; gold has density 19 300 kg/m³. V = m ÷ ρ = 12.5 ÷ 19 300 = 6.5 × 10⁻⁴ m³ (650 cm³).\n\n### Situation: will it float?\n\n**Example — comparing densities.** A wooden block has mass 300 g and volume 400 cm³.\n\n- ρ = 300 ÷ 400 = 0.75 g/cm³, which is less than water's 1.0 g/cm³, so it floats.\n- (Supplement) In a column of syrup (1.4 g/cm³), water (1.0 g/cm³) and oil (0.9 g/cm³), the liquids settle in that order from the bottom: syrup, water, oil. The block (0.75 g/cm³) floats on top of the oil.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Mass in kg, weight in N. Never give weight in kg.\n- Mass does not change from place to place; weight does, because g changes.\n- Match density units: g and cm³ give g/cm³; kg and m³ give kg/m³.\n- For displacement, the volume is the difference between the two readings, not the final reading.\n- To decide floating, compare densities, not masses: a huge ship floats and a small stone sinks.\n",
  "igcse-u4": "# Unit 4 — Forces and their effects\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 1.5.1, 1.5.2 and 1.5.3, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Forces change shape and motion — 1.5.1\n\nA force can change the size and shape of an object (stretch, squash, bend, twist) and can change its motion. A resultant force changes an object's velocity: it can speed it up, slow it down or change its direction.\n\nIf the resultant force is zero, an object at rest stays at rest, and a moving object carries on in a straight line at constant speed. (This is Newton's first law.) A car cruising at a steady 30 m/s on a straight road has a driving force exactly balanced by friction and air resistance.\n\n### Resultant of forces along a line — 1.5.1\n\nForces acting along the same line add if they point the same way and subtract if they point in opposite directions. Choose one direction as positive. A 50 N push forward and a 20 N friction backward give a resultant of 30 N forward.\n\n### Friction and drag — 1.5.1\n\nSolid friction is the force between two surfaces that may impede (oppose) motion and produces heating; rub your hands together and they get warm. Drag is friction on an object moving through a liquid (water resistance) or a gas (air resistance). Drag increases as the speed increases.\n\n### Stretching springs — 1.5.1\n\nFor a load–extension experiment, clamp a spring above a ruler, record the position of the bottom of the spring, add masses one at a time and record the new position each time. Extension = new length − original length, never the total length. Weight of each load = mg.\n\nThe graph of load against extension is a straight line through the origin at first: the extension is proportional to the load (Hooke's law). **Supplement:** the point where the line stops being straight is the limit of proportionality. Beyond it, extension is no longer proportional to load.\n\n**Supplement:** the spring constant k is the force per unit extension. A stiff spring has a large k. On a load (y-axis) against extension (x-axis) graph, k is the gradient of the straight part.\n\n### Force, mass and acceleration — 1.5.1 (Supplement)\n\nA resultant force F on a mass m produces an acceleration a in the same direction as the force: F = ma. Double the resultant force and the acceleration doubles; double the mass and the acceleration halves. Always use the resultant force, not just the driving force.\n\n### Motion in a circle — 1.5.1 (Supplement)\n\nAn object moving in a circle at constant speed is accelerating, because its direction keeps changing. It needs a force perpendicular to its motion, towards the centre of the circle (tension in a string, friction on car tyres, gravity on a satellite). If the force stops, the object moves off in a straight line along the tangent.\n\n- With mass and radius fixed, a larger force means a higher speed.\n- With mass and speed fixed, a larger force means a smaller radius.\n- With speed and radius fixed, a larger mass needs a larger force.\n\n(The formula F = mv²/r is not required.)\n\n### Turning effect: moments — 1.5.2\n\nThe moment of a force is its turning effect about a pivot: moment = force × perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of action of the force. Unit: N m. Everyday examples: a spanner (a longer handle gives a bigger moment for the same force), a door handle placed far from the hinges, a see-saw, a wheelbarrow.\n\nPrinciple of moments: for an object in equilibrium, the sum of the clockwise moments about any point equals the sum of the anticlockwise moments about that point.\n\nAn object is in equilibrium when there is no resultant force and no resultant moment. Then it neither starts moving nor starts turning.\n\n**Supplement — experiment:** balance a metre rule on a pivot at its centre. Hang different weights at measured distances on each side until the rule balances again. For each weight calculate force × distance; the total clockwise moment equals the total anticlockwise moment, showing there is no resultant moment.\n\n### Centre of gravity and stability — 1.5.3\n\nThe centre of gravity is the point through which the whole weight of an object can be taken to act.\n\nTo find it for an irregular flat sheet (lamina): make three small holes near the edge. Hang the lamina from a pin through one hole so it swings freely, hang a plumb line from the same pin, and draw along the plumb line. Repeat for the other holes. The centre of gravity is where the lines cross (the third line checks the first two).\n\nAn object is stable when its centre of gravity is low and its base is wide. If it is tilted, its weight acts through the base and produces a moment that turns it back. It topples when it is tilted so far that the line of action of its weight falls outside the base. Racing cars are low and wide; a double-decker bus keeps heavy parts low.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| resultant = sum of forces in one direction − sum in the opposite direction | N | Forces along one straight line. | Core |\n| extension = stretched length − original length | m or cm | Every spring or rubber-band question. | Core |\n| moment = F × d | F: force, N; d: perpendicular distance from pivot, m; moment: N m | Turning effect of one force. | Core |\n| sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments | N m | Balanced beams and see-saws in equilibrium (Core: one force each side; Supplement: several). | Core |\n| k = F ÷ x | k: spring constant, N/m (or N/cm); F: load, N; x: extension, m | Up to the limit of proportionality only. Rearranged: F = kx, x = F ÷ k. | Supplement |\n| F = ma | F: resultant force, N; m: mass, kg; a: acceleration, m/s² | Resultant force and acceleration, same direction. Rearranged: a = F ÷ m, m = F ÷ a. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: forces along a line\n\n**Example — tug of war.** Team A pulls left with 820 N; team B pulls right with 760 N. Resultant = 820 − 760 = 60 N to the left, so the rope accelerates to the left.\n\n**Example — steady speed.** A lorry moves at constant velocity with a driving force of 4500 N. The total resistive force must also be 4500 N, because constant velocity means zero resultant force.\n\n### Situation: springs (Supplement for k)\n\n**Example — spring constant.** A spring of length 12.0 cm stretches to 15.0 cm under a 6.0 N load.\n\n- Extension x = 15.0 − 12.0 = 3.0 cm = 0.030 m.\n- k = F ÷ x = 6.0 ÷ 0.030 = 200 N/m.\n\n**Example — predict an extension.** The same spring has a 4.0 N load. x = F ÷ k = 4.0 ÷ 200 = 0.020 m = 2.0 cm (as long as the limit of proportionality is not passed).\n\n**Example — from the graph.** The straight part of a load–extension graph passes through (0.050 m, 2.5 N). Gradient = k = 2.5 ÷ 0.050 = 50 N/m.\n\n### Situation: F = ma with one force (Supplement)\n\n**Example — acceleration.** A resultant force of 1200 N acts on a 800 kg car. a = 1200 ÷ 800 = 1.5 m/s².\n\n**Example — force needed.** A 0.15 kg ball is to be given an acceleration of 40 m/s². F = 0.15 × 40 = 6.0 N.\n\n### Situation: F = ma with friction (Supplement)\n\n**Example — resultant first.** A 50 kg crate is pushed with 180 N against 80 N of friction.\n\n- Resultant = 180 − 80 = 100 N.\n- a = 100 ÷ 50 = 2.0 m/s².\n\n**Example — braking force.** A 1000 kg car decelerates from 20 m/s to rest in 4.0 s.\n\n- a = (0 − 20) ÷ 4.0 = −5.0 m/s².\n- F = ma = 1000 × (−5.0) = −5000 N: a 5000 N braking force opposite to the motion.\n\n**Example — lift or rocket.** A 2000 kg rocket has a thrust of 30 000 N (g = 9.8 N/kg).\n\n- Weight = 2000 × 9.8 = 19 600 N.\n- Resultant upward force = 30 000 − 19 600 = 10 400 N.\n- a = 10 400 ÷ 2000 = 5.2 m/s² upward.\n\n### Situation: one force each side of a pivot\n\n**Example — see-saw.** A 400 N child sits 1.5 m from the pivot. Where must a 300 N child sit to balance?\n\n- Anticlockwise moment = 400 × 1.5 = 600 N m.\n- 300 × d = 600, so d = 2.0 m on the other side.\n\n**Example — spanner.** A 25 N force at the end of a 0.20 m spanner gives a moment of 25 × 0.20 = 5.0 N m. Holding it at 0.10 m would need 50 N for the same turning effect.\n\n### Situation: several forces each side (Supplement)\n\n**Example — metre rule.** A uniform metre rule is pivoted at its centre (50 cm mark). A 2.0 N weight hangs at the 10 cm mark and a 3.0 N weight at the 30 cm mark. Where must a 5.0 N weight hang on the other side?\n\n- Anticlockwise moments: 2.0 × 0.40 + 3.0 × 0.20 = 0.80 + 0.60 = 1.40 N m.\n- Clockwise: 5.0 × d = 1.40, so d = 0.28 m from the pivot, at the 78 cm mark.\n\n**Example — beam with its own weight.** A uniform 4.0 m plank weighing 200 N rests on a pivot 1.0 m from its left end. What downward force at the left end balances it?\n\n- The plank's weight acts at its centre, 2.0 m from the left end, which is 1.0 m to the right of the pivot: clockwise moment = 200 × 1.0 = 200 N m.\n- Force at the left end, 1.0 m from the pivot: F × 1.0 = 200, so F = 200 N.\n\n**Example — force on the pivot.** In equilibrium the upward force from the pivot equals the total downward force (no resultant force). For the plank above, the pivot pushes up with 200 + 200 = 400 N.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Use the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of the force, in metres for N m.\n- In a moments question, take moments about the pivot so the pivot's own force has no moment.\n- Equilibrium needs both: no resultant force and no resultant moment.\n- Extension is the increase in length, not the new length.\n- F in F = ma is the resultant force. Subtract friction or weight first.\n- Explain toppling with the line of action of the weight falling outside the base.\n",
  "igcse-u5": "# Unit 5 — Momentum\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 1.6, Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Momentum — 1.6 (Supplement)\n\nMomentum is mass × velocity. It is a vector, so it has a direction; choose one direction as positive and give velocities in the other direction a minus sign. Unit: kg m/s. A lorry and a bicycle moving at the same velocity have very different momenta, because the lorry's mass is far greater.\n\n### Impulse — 1.6 (Supplement)\n\nImpulse is force × the time for which the force acts. An impulse changes an object's momentum by exactly that amount: impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv). Unit: N s, which is the same as kg m/s.\n\nThe same change in momentum can come from a large force for a short time or a small force for a long time. Safety features use this: crumple zones, airbags, seat belts, crash mats, cycle helmets and bending your knees when you land all increase the time over which the momentum changes, so the force is smaller.\n\n### Resultant force and momentum — 1.6 (Supplement)\n\nResultant force is the change in momentum per unit time: F = Δp ÷ Δt. This is another form of F = ma, because Δp ÷ Δt = m(Δv ÷ Δt) = ma when the mass is constant.\n\n### Conservation of momentum — 1.6 (Supplement)\n\nWhen objects collide or push apart (explode), the total momentum before equals the total momentum after, provided no external resultant force acts on them. Kinetic energy is usually not conserved in a collision (some becomes internal energy and sound), but momentum always is.\n\n- Collision where the objects stick together: they move off with one shared velocity.\n- Collision where they bounce: each has its own velocity afterwards; one may reverse.\n- Explosion or recoil from rest: total momentum before is zero, so afterwards the momenta are equal in size and opposite in direction. A gun recoils when it fires a bullet; a skater pushing off another skater moves backward.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| p = mv | p: momentum, kg m/s; m: mass, kg; v: velocity, m/s | Momentum of one object. Keep the sign of v. | Supplement |\n| impulse = FΔt = Δ(mv) | F: force, N; Δt: time the force acts, s; Δ(mv) = mv − mu, kg m/s or N s | A force acting for a time, such as a kick, a crash or a catch. | Supplement |\n| F = Δp ÷ Δt | F: resultant force, N; Δp: change in momentum, kg m/s; Δt: time taken, s | Average force during an impact, or force from a momentum change. | Supplement |\n| total momentum before = total momentum after | m₁u₁ + m₂u₂ = m₁v₁ + m₂v₂ (u before, v after) | Collisions and explosions with no external resultant force. | Supplement |\n\nΔp is always final momentum minus initial momentum. If an object bounces back, its final velocity has the opposite sign, so the change is bigger than either momentum alone.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: momentum of one object\n\n**Example — running.** A 60 kg student runs at 5.0 m/s. p = 60 × 5.0 = 300 kg m/s in the direction of running.\n\n**Example — find the speed.** A 0.20 kg puck has momentum 1.2 kg m/s. v = p ÷ m = 1.2 ÷ 0.20 = 6.0 m/s.\n\n### Situation: impulse and average force\n\n**Example — kicking a ball.** A 0.45 kg football at rest is kicked to 20 m/s. The boot is in contact for 0.015 s.\n\n- Δp = 0.45 × 20 − 0 = 9.0 kg m/s (the impulse is 9.0 N s).\n- F = Δp ÷ Δt = 9.0 ÷ 0.015 = 600 N.\n\n**Example — a ball that bounces.** A 0.10 kg ball hits a wall at 8.0 m/s and rebounds at 6.0 m/s. Contact time 0.020 s. Take towards the wall as positive.\n\n- Before: 0.10 × 8.0 = +0.80 kg m/s. After: 0.10 × (−6.0) = −0.60 kg m/s.\n- Δp = −0.60 − 0.80 = −1.40 kg m/s (size 1.4 kg m/s, away from the wall).\n- F = 1.40 ÷ 0.020 = 70 N, away from the wall.\n\n### Situation: safety features (longer stopping time)\n\n**Example — airbag.** A 70 kg driver moving at 15 m/s is stopped in a crash.\n\n- Δp = 70 × 15 = 1050 kg m/s whatever stops them.\n- Stopped by a dashboard in 0.010 s: F = 1050 ÷ 0.010 = 105 000 N.\n- Stopped by an airbag in 0.15 s: F = 1050 ÷ 0.15 = 7000 N, fifteen times smaller.\n\n### Situation: collision, objects stick together\n\n**Example — trolleys.** A 2.0 kg trolley at 3.0 m/s hits a stationary 1.0 kg trolley and they join.\n\n- Before: 2.0 × 3.0 + 1.0 × 0 = 6.0 kg m/s.\n- After: (2.0 + 1.0) × v = 6.0, so v = 2.0 m/s in the original direction.\n\n### Situation: head-on collision, objects moving towards each other\n\n**Example — opposite directions.** A 3.0 kg ball moving right at 4.0 m/s hits a 1.0 kg ball moving left at 2.0 m/s. They stick together. Right is positive.\n\n- Before: 3.0 × 4.0 + 1.0 × (−2.0) = 12 − 2 = 10 kg m/s.\n- After: 4.0 × v = 10, so v = 2.5 m/s to the right.\n\n### Situation: collision, objects bounce apart\n\n**Example — find one velocity.** A 0.50 kg ball at 6.0 m/s hits a stationary 1.5 kg ball. Afterwards the 1.5 kg ball moves forward at 2.5 m/s.\n\n- Before: 0.50 × 6.0 = 3.0 kg m/s.\n- After: 0.50 × v + 1.5 × 2.5 = 3.0, so 0.50v = 3.0 − 3.75 = −0.75, and v = −1.5 m/s.\n- The minus sign means the 0.50 kg ball bounces back at 1.5 m/s.\n\n### Situation: explosion or recoil from rest\n\n**Example — rifle.** A 4.0 kg rifle fires a 0.010 kg bullet at 400 m/s.\n\n- Total momentum before = 0.\n- After: 0.010 × 400 + 4.0 × v = 0, so 4.0v = −4.0, and v = −1.0 m/s.\n- The rifle recoils at 1.0 m/s, opposite to the bullet.\n\n**Example — skaters.** Two skaters of 50 kg and 75 kg stand together and push apart. The 50 kg skater moves off at 3.0 m/s. The 75 kg skater moves the other way at (50 × 3.0) ÷ 75 = 2.0 m/s.\n\n### Situation: force from a momentum change per second\n\n**Example — water jet.** A hose sends 2.0 kg of water each second at 10 m/s onto a wall, where it stops. Δp per second = 2.0 × 10 = 20 kg m/s each second, so the force on the wall is 20 N.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Choose a positive direction and keep signs on every velocity.\n- Momentum is conserved in every collision and explosion with no external force; kinetic energy usually is not.\n- In a stick-together collision, use the combined mass after.\n- An explosion from rest has zero total momentum: equal and opposite momenta.\n- Explain safety features in words: longer time for the same change in momentum gives a smaller force (F = Δp ÷ Δt).\n- N s and kg m/s are the same unit.\n",
  "igcse-u6": "# Unit 6 — Energy, work and power\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 1.7.1, 1.7.2, 1.7.3 and 1.7.4, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Energy stores and transfers — 1.7.1\n\nEnergy can be stored as kinetic, gravitational potential, chemical, elastic (strain), nuclear, electrostatic and internal (thermal) energy.\n\nEnergy is transferred between stores in four ways:\n\n- by forces doing mechanical work (a crane lifting a load);\n- by electrical currents doing electrical work (a battery driving a motor);\n- by heating (a hot plate warming a pan);\n- by waves: electromagnetic, sound and others (sunlight warming the ground, a loudspeaker).\n\n**Examples of transfers.** A falling ball: gravitational potential → kinetic. A car braking: kinetic → internal (in the hot brakes). A battery-powered torch: chemical → (electrical work) → light and internal. A bow and arrow: elastic → kinetic.\n\n### Conservation of energy — 1.7.1\n\nEnergy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred from one store to another. The total energy after any event equals the total before. Some energy always ends up as \"wasted\" internal energy of the surroundings, often through friction or electrical resistance; it is not lost, just spread out and no longer useful.\n\nA flow (Sankey) diagram shows this: the width of each arrow is proportional to the energy. The input arrow is as wide as all the output arrows together. **Supplement:** apply this to processes with several stages, such as a power station (chemical → internal → kinetic of turbine → electrical), and read values off a Sankey diagram drawn to scale.\n\n### Kinetic and gravitational potential energy — 1.7.1 (Supplement)\n\nKinetic energy depends on mass and the square of speed: double the speed and the kinetic energy becomes four times as large. The change in gravitational potential energy depends on mass, g and the change in height.\n\nWhen an object falls with negligible air resistance, the loss in gravitational potential energy equals the gain in kinetic energy. When there is resistance, part of the potential energy becomes internal energy instead.\n\n### Work — 1.7.2\n\nWork done is the energy transferred. Mechanical work is done when a force moves an object in the direction of the force. Electrical work is done when a current transfers energy. 1 joule is the work done when a 1 N force moves an object 1 m in the direction of the force.\n\nNo work is done if there is no movement in the direction of the force: holding a heavy bag still does no work on the bag.\n\n### Energy resources — 1.7.3\n\n| Resource | How the energy is obtained | Renewable? | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Fossil fuels (coal, oil, gas) | Burned to boil water in a boiler; steam turns a turbine, which turns a generator | No | Reliable and available on demand; release carbon dioxide (climate change) and other pollutants. |\n| Biofuels (wood, ethanol, biogas) | Burned like fossil fuels | Yes | Plants absorb carbon dioxide as they grow; need large areas of land. |\n| Hydroelectric dams | Water stored behind a dam flows down through turbines | Yes | Reliable, quick to start; floods valleys and damages habitats; needs suitable sites. |\n| Tides | Tidal water flows through turbines in a barrage | Yes | Predictable, twice a day; few suitable estuaries, affects wildlife. |\n| Waves | Up-and-down motion of waves drives a generator | Yes | Small scale, unreliable (depends on weather), can be a hazard to boats. |\n| Geothermal | Hot rocks underground heat water to steam, which turns a turbine | Yes | Reliable; only in volcanic areas; drilling is costly. |\n| Nuclear fuel | Fission of uranium heats water to steam for a turbine | No | Large scale and reliable, no carbon dioxide; radioactive waste, risk of accidents, costly to build and decommission. |\n| Solar cells | Light is converted directly to electrical energy | Yes | No moving parts, no pollution in use; nothing at night, less on cloudy days, small output per area. |\n| Solar panels | Infrared from the Sun heats water flowing through them | Yes | Good for hot water; depends on sunshine. |\n| Wind | Wind turns turbine blades connected to a generator | Yes | No fuel or pollution in use; unreliable, noisy, visual impact, many needed. |\n\nEfficiency is how much of the input energy ends up in the useful form. No device is 100% efficient.\n\n**Supplement:** radiation from the Sun is the main source of energy for all our resources except geothermal, nuclear and tidal. (Fossil fuels and biofuels stored the Sun's energy through photosynthesis; the Sun's heating drives wind, waves and the water cycle that fills dams.) The Sun releases energy by nuclear fusion, and research is being carried out into fusion power stations that could generate electrical energy on a large scale.\n\n### Power — 1.7.4\n\nPower is the work done per unit time, or the energy transferred per unit time. Unit: watt (W); 1 W = 1 J/s. A 2 kW kettle transfers 2000 J every second.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| W = Fd = ΔE | W: work done, J; F: force, N; d: distance moved in the direction of the force, m; ΔE: energy transferred, J | Any force moving an object; lifting, pushing, braking. | Core |\n| P = W ÷ t | P: power, W; W: work done, J; t: time, s | Power from work done. Rearranged: W = Pt. | Core |\n| P = ΔE ÷ t | ΔE: energy transferred, J | Power from energy transferred (heaters, lamps, motors). | Core |\n| Eₖ = ½mv² | Eₖ: kinetic energy, J; m: mass, kg; v: speed, m/s | Energy of a moving object. Rearranged: v = √(2Eₖ ÷ m). | Supplement |\n| ΔEₚ = mgΔh | ΔEₚ: change in gravitational potential energy, J; m: kg; g: 9.8 N/kg; Δh: change in height, m | Raising or lowering an object near the Earth's surface. | Supplement |\n| % efficiency = (useful energy output ÷ total energy input) × 100% | Both energies in J | Energy form of efficiency. | Supplement |\n| % efficiency = (useful power output ÷ total power input) × 100% | Both powers in W | Power form of efficiency. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: work done by a force\n\n**Example — pushing a box.** A 60 N force pushes a box 4.0 m along the floor. W = 60 × 4.0 = 240 J.\n\n**Example — lifting.** Lifting a 50 N bag onto a shelf 1.6 m high: the force needed equals the weight, so W = 50 × 1.6 = 80 J.\n\n**Example — braking distance.** A car's brakes exert 6000 N and do 180 000 J of work to stop it. d = W ÷ F = 180 000 ÷ 6000 = 30 m.\n\n### Situation: power\n\n**Example — motor.** A motor lifts a 300 N load through 12 m in 20 s.\n\n- W = 300 × 12 = 3600 J.\n- P = 3600 ÷ 20 = 180 W.\n\n**Example — energy used.** A 1500 W hair dryer runs for 5 minutes. E = Pt = 1500 × 300 = 450 000 J = 450 kJ.\n\n**Example — running upstairs.** A 600 N student climbs 3.0 m of stairs in 4.0 s. Power = (600 × 3.0) ÷ 4.0 = 450 W.\n\n### Situation: kinetic energy (Supplement)\n\n**Example — car.** A 900 kg car moves at 20 m/s. Eₖ = ½ × 900 × 20² = 180 000 J.\n\n**Example — doubling the speed.** At 40 m/s the same car has ½ × 900 × 40² = 720 000 J: four times as much.\n\n**Example — find the speed.** A 0.50 kg ball has 64 J of kinetic energy. v² = 2 × 64 ÷ 0.50 = 256, so v = 16 m/s.\n\n### Situation: gravitational potential energy (Supplement)\n\n**Example — lifting a box.** A 15 kg box is lifted 2.0 m. ΔEₚ = 15 × 9.8 × 2.0 = 294 J.\n\n### Situation: falling and swinging, energy conservation (Supplement)\n\n**Example — dropped object.** A ball is dropped from 5.0 m. Ignoring air resistance, what is its speed when it hits the ground?\n\n- Loss of Eₚ = gain of Eₖ, so mgΔh = ½mv². The mass cancels.\n- v² = 2gΔh = 2 × 9.8 × 5.0 = 98, so v = 9.9 m/s.\n\n**Example — pendulum or roller coaster.** A roller coaster car starts at rest 20 m above the lowest point. Ignoring friction: v² = 2 × 9.8 × 20 = 392, so v = 19.8 m/s at the bottom.\n\n**Example — how high does it go?** A ball is thrown straight up at 12 m/s. At the top, all the kinetic energy has become potential energy: Δh = v² ÷ 2g = 144 ÷ 19.6 = 7.3 m.\n\n**Example — with friction.** A 40 kg child slides down a slide 3.0 m high and reaches the bottom at 6.0 m/s.\n\n- Eₚ lost = 40 × 9.8 × 3.0 = 1176 J.\n- Eₖ gained = ½ × 40 × 6.0² = 720 J.\n- Energy transferred to internal energy by friction = 1176 − 720 = 456 J.\n\n### Situation: efficiency (Supplement)\n\n**Example — lamp.** A lamp is supplied with 200 J and gives out 30 J of light. Efficiency = (30 ÷ 200) × 100% = 15%. The other 170 J heats the surroundings.\n\n**Example — motor.** A motor has an input power of 800 W and an efficiency of 75%. Useful output power = 0.75 × 800 = 600 W.\n\n**Example — find the input.** A power station delivers 400 MW of electrical power at 40% efficiency. Input power = 400 ÷ 0.40 = 1000 MW, so 600 MW is wasted, mostly heating the surroundings.\n\n**Example — Sankey diagram.** An arrow for 500 J input splits into 350 J useful and 150 J wasted. Efficiency = 350 ÷ 500 × 100% = 70%. In a scale drawing, the 350 J arrow is 70% of the width of the input arrow.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Name the energy stores precisely: \"gravitational potential\", \"kinetic\", \"internal (thermal)\"; avoid \"movement energy\" and \"heat energy\".\n- Work uses the distance moved in the direction of the force.\n- Convert to J, s, kg and m/s first: kJ × 1000, minutes × 60, g ÷ 1000.\n- In Eₖ = ½mv² only the speed is squared. Square it before multiplying.\n- In energy-conservation questions, mass often cancels; say which energy store loses and which gains.\n- Efficiency can never be more than 100%, and it has no unit.\n- For an energy resource, compare it on renewability, availability, reliability, scale and environmental impact.\n",
  "igcse-u7": "# Unit 7 — Pressure\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 1.8, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Pressure on a surface — 1.8\n\nPressure is the force per unit area, acting at right angles (perpendicular) to a surface. Unit: pascal (Pa); 1 Pa = 1 N/m².\n\nFor the same force, a smaller area gives a larger pressure; a larger area gives a smaller pressure.\n\n- Large pressure wanted (small area): a sharp knife, a needle, a drawing pin's point, football studs, the narrow heel of a shoe that dents a floor.\n- Small pressure wanted (large area): snowshoes and skis, wide tyres on tractors, the wide head of a drawing pin against your thumb, the foundations of a building, a camel's wide feet on sand.\n\n### Pressure in liquids — 1.8\n\nPressure beneath the surface of a liquid:\n\n- increases with depth, because there is a greater weight of liquid above;\n- increases with the density of the liquid;\n- acts equally in all directions at a given depth;\n- does not depend on the shape or width of the container.\n\nThis is why a dam is built thicker at the bottom, why water spurts furthest from the lowest hole in a can, and why divers' ears hurt as they go deeper.\n\n**Supplement:** the change in pressure between two depths is the density × g × the change in depth. At the surface the pressure is atmospheric pressure (about 100 000 Pa), so the total pressure at a depth is atmospheric pressure plus the liquid's pressure.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| p = F ÷ A | p: pressure, Pa (N/m²); F: force perpendicular to the surface, N; A: area, m² | Solids pressing on surfaces; force on an area from a known pressure: F = pA. | Core |\n| Δp = ρgΔh | Δp: change in pressure, Pa; ρ: density of the liquid, kg/m³; g: 9.8 N/kg; Δh: change in depth, m | Pressure caused by a column of liquid. | Supplement |\n| total pressure at depth = atmospheric pressure + ρgh | Pa | When the question asks for the total (absolute) pressure, not just the extra pressure from the liquid. | Supplement |\n\nArea conversions: 1 m² = 10 000 cm² (100 × 100) and 1 cm² = 0.0001 m². This is the most common mistake in pressure questions.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: an object standing on a surface\n\n**Example — block in different orientations.** A block of weight 60 N measures 0.10 m × 0.20 m × 0.30 m. What are the largest and smallest pressures it can exert on the floor?\n\n- Smallest area: 0.10 × 0.20 = 0.020 m². Largest pressure = 60 ÷ 0.020 = 3000 Pa.\n- Largest area: 0.20 × 0.30 = 0.060 m². Smallest pressure = 60 ÷ 0.060 = 1000 Pa.\n\n**Example — area in cm².** A 700 N person stands on both feet with a total contact area of 350 cm².\n\n- A = 350 cm² = 350 ÷ 10 000 = 0.035 m².\n- p = 700 ÷ 0.035 = 20 000 Pa.\n- On one foot the area halves and the pressure doubles to 40 000 Pa.\n\n**Example — weight from mass.** A 1200 kg car rests on four tyres, each with 0.015 m² in contact with the road.\n\n- F = W = 1200 × 9.8 = 11 760 N.\n- A = 4 × 0.015 = 0.060 m².\n- p = 11 760 ÷ 0.060 = 196 000 Pa.\n\n### Situation: find the force or the area\n\n**Example — force from pressure.** Air pressure of 100 000 Pa acts on a window of area 1.5 m². F = pA = 100 000 × 1.5 = 150 000 N. (It does not break because the same pressure pushes from the other side.)\n\n**Example — area needed.** A building of weight 2.0 × 10⁷ N stands on ground that can take at most 400 000 Pa. A = F ÷ p = 2.0 × 10⁷ ÷ 400 000 = 50 m² of foundations.\n\n### Situation: pressure in a liquid (Supplement)\n\n**Example — swimming pool.** What is the pressure due to the water at the bottom of a 2.5 m deep pool? (Water density 1000 kg/m³.)\n\n- Δp = 1000 × 9.8 × 2.5 = 24 500 Pa.\n- Total pressure there = 100 000 + 24 500 = 124 500 Pa.\n\n**Example — between two depths.** A diver descends from 5.0 m to 15 m in sea water (density 1030 kg/m³). Δp = 1030 × 9.8 × 10 = 100 940 Pa: roughly one extra atmosphere for every 10 m.\n\n**Example — find the depth.** A sensor in a lake reads an extra pressure of 49 000 Pa. Δh = Δp ÷ ρg = 49 000 ÷ (1000 × 9.8) = 5.0 m.\n\n**Example — different liquid.** At the same 0.20 m depth, mercury (13 600 kg/m³) gives 13 600 × 9.8 × 0.20 = 26 656 Pa, but water gives only 1000 × 9.8 × 0.20 = 1960 Pa. Greater density, greater pressure.\n\n### Situation: force on a submerged surface (Supplement)\n\n**Example — submarine hatch.** A hatch of area 0.50 m² is 40 m below the surface of sea water (1030 kg/m³).\n\n- Pressure from the water = 1030 × 9.8 × 40 = 403 760 Pa.\n- Force = pA = 403 760 × 0.50 = 201 880 N, about 2.0 × 10⁵ N.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Area must be in m² for an answer in Pa. Divide cm² by 10 000.\n- The force in p = F ÷ A is the force perpendicular to the surface; for an object resting on the ground it is its weight, mg.\n- Liquid pressure depends on depth and density only, not on the container's shape or the total volume.\n- Δh is the vertical depth below the surface, not the distance along a slope.\n- Use \"increases with depth because the weight of liquid above increases\" for describe-and-explain questions.\n",
  "igcse-u8": "# Unit 8 — Kinetic particle model of matter\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 2.1.1, 2.1.2 and 2.1.3, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### States of matter — 2.1.1\n\n| Property | Solid | Liquid | Gas |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shape | Fixed | Takes the shape of the container | Fills the whole container |\n| Volume | Fixed | Fixed | Fills the container; easily changed |\n| Compressible? | No (hardly) | No (hardly) | Yes, easily |\n| Flows? | No | Yes | Yes |\n\nChanges of state: melting (solid → liquid), boiling or evaporation (liquid → gas), condensation (gas → liquid), solidification or freezing (liquid → solid).\n\n### The particle model — 2.1.2\n\n- **Solid:** particles closely packed in a regular arrangement (a lattice). They vibrate about fixed positions. Strong forces between them.\n- **Liquid:** particles close together but irregularly arranged. They move around each other (slide past one another). Forces are weaker than in a solid.\n- **Gas:** particles far apart (about ten times further than in a liquid), randomly arranged, moving quickly in random directions in straight lines between collisions. Forces between them are very small.\n\nIn a particle diagram, draw solids as touching circles in neat rows, liquids as mostly touching circles with gaps and no pattern, and gases as a few widely spaced circles.\n\n**Supplement:** the forces and distances between particles (atoms, molecules, ions, electrons) and the way the particles move explain the properties. Strong forces and fixed positions give a solid its fixed shape; particles that can move past each other let a liquid flow; large spaces between gas particles make a gas easy to compress.\n\n### Temperature and particle motion — 2.1.2\n\nThe higher the temperature, the faster the particles move on average: temperature is linked to the average kinetic energy of the particles. As a substance is cooled, its particles slow down. At absolute zero, −273 °C, the particles have the least possible kinetic energy. Nothing can be colder.\n\n### Gas pressure — 2.1.2\n\nGas particles constantly collide with the walls of their container. Each collision exerts a small force on the wall; the many collisions together produce the pressure.\n\n**Supplement:** each particle changes momentum when it rebounds from the wall, so it exerts a force on the wall. The total force from all the collisions divided by the wall's area is the pressure (force per unit area).\n\n### Brownian motion — 2.1.2\n\nSmoke particles in air (or pollen grains in water) seen through a microscope move in a jerky, random, zigzag way. This is evidence for the kinetic particle model: the visible particles are being hit unevenly, from all sides, by the much smaller, fast-moving air or water molecules that cannot be seen.\n\n**Supplement:** the air molecules are light and fast. A large, heavy smoke particle is moved only slightly by each collision, but at any instant more molecules hit one side than the other, so it is pushed in a changing, random direction. Use \"molecules\" or \"atoms\" for the air or water, and \"microscopic particles\" for the smoke or pollen.\n\n### Gases: pressure, volume and temperature — 2.1.3\n\nFor a fixed mass of gas:\n\n- **Temperature up, volume fixed → pressure up.** The particles move faster, hit the walls more often and harder, so the force per unit area increases.\n- **Volume down, temperature fixed → pressure up.** The particles are closer together and hit the walls more often (the average speed is unchanged), so the pressure increases.\n\nThe kelvin (absolute) scale starts at absolute zero: 0 K = −273 °C. A change of 1 K is the same size as a change of 1 °C.\n\n**Supplement:** at constant temperature, pressure × volume is constant (Boyle's law). Halve the volume and the pressure doubles. A graph of p against V is a curve (inverse proportion); a graph of p against 1/V is a straight line through the origin.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| T = θ + 273 | T: temperature, K; θ: temperature, °C | Converting °C to kelvin. Backwards: θ = T − 273. | Core |\n| pV = constant | p: pressure, Pa (or any pressure unit); V: volume, m³ (or any volume unit) | Fixed mass of gas at constant temperature. | Supplement |\n| p₁V₁ = p₂V₂ | 1: before the change; 2: after | The working form of pV = constant. The units only need to match on both sides. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: converting temperatures\n\n**Example — Celsius to kelvin.** Water boils at 100 °C = 100 + 273 = 373 K. Body temperature 37 °C = 310 K.\n\n**Example — kelvin to Celsius.** Liquid nitrogen boils at 77 K = 77 − 273 = −196 °C.\n\n**Example — a temperature change.** Heating from 20 °C to 50 °C is a rise of 30 °C, which is also a rise of 30 K. Do not add 273 to a change.\n\n### Situation: compressing a gas (Supplement)\n\n**Example — find the new pressure.** A syringe holds 60 cm³ of air at 100 kPa. The plunger is pushed in slowly (constant temperature) until the volume is 20 cm³.\n\n- p₁V₁ = p₂V₂: 100 × 60 = p₂ × 20.\n- p₂ = 6000 ÷ 20 = 300 kPa. A third of the volume gives three times the pressure.\n\n### Situation: a gas that expands (Supplement)\n\n**Example — rising bubble.** A bubble of volume 2.0 cm³ is released at the bottom of a lake where the pressure is 250 kPa. At the surface the pressure is 100 kPa. Assume constant temperature.\n\n- 250 × 2.0 = 100 × V₂, so V₂ = 5.0 cm³. The bubble grows as the pressure falls.\n\n### Situation: find the original volume (Supplement)\n\n**Example — gas cylinder.** Gas from a cylinder of volume 0.050 m³ at 2 000 000 Pa is released into the air at 100 000 Pa (same temperature). What volume does it take up?\n\n- V₂ = p₁V₁ ÷ p₂ = 2 000 000 × 0.050 ÷ 100 000 = 1.0 m³.\n\n### Situation: reading a pV graph (Supplement)\n\n**Example — check the law.** Readings: (p = 100 kPa, V = 30 cm³), (150 kPa, 20 cm³), (300 kPa, 10 cm³). Each product pV = 3000 kPa cm³, so pressure is inversely proportional to volume.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Describe particles with three ideas: arrangement, separation, motion.\n- Explain gas pressure with collisions with the walls: more frequent and harder collisions mean a larger force per unit area.\n- Compressing at constant temperature: the collisions become more frequent, not harder.\n- Brownian motion: the moving thing you see is the smoke particle, hit by unseen air molecules.\n- Use pV = constant only when the mass of gas and the temperature are both fixed.\n- Absolute zero is −273 °C, which is 0 K.\n",
  "igcse-u9": "# Unit 9 — Thermal properties and temperature\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 2.2.1, 2.2.2 and 2.2.3, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Thermal expansion — 2.2.1\n\nMost materials expand when heated (at constant pressure) and contract when cooled. For the same temperature rise, gases expand most, liquids less, and solids least.\n\nEveryday applications and consequences:\n\n- Gaps are left between sections of railway track and in bridges (expansion joints) so they do not buckle in hot weather.\n- Overhead power cables are hung with some slack so they do not snap when they contract in cold weather.\n- A liquid-in-glass thermometer works because the liquid expands up the narrow tube as it warms.\n- A bimetallic strip (two metals that expand by different amounts) bends when heated; it is used in thermostats and fire alarms.\n- A tight metal lid on a glass jar can be loosened by running it under hot water.\n\n**Supplement:** in a solid the particles are held tightly by strong forces, so extra vibration pushes them only slightly further apart: small expansion. In a liquid the forces are weaker, so the particles move further apart: more expansion. In a gas the forces are almost zero, so the particles spread out freely: by far the largest expansion.\n\n### Internal energy and specific heat capacity — 2.2.2\n\nA rise in the temperature of an object increases its internal energy.\n\n**Supplement:** a temperature rise means an increase in the average kinetic energy of all the particles in the object.\n\n**Supplement:** specific heat capacity, c, is the energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 kg of a substance by 1 °C. Water has a high value (4200 J/(kg °C)), so it takes a lot of energy to heat and stores a lot; that is why it is used in central heating and car cooling systems. Metals have low values (copper about 390 J/(kg °C)), so they heat up quickly.\n\n**Supplement — measuring c of a solid:** use a metal block with two holes, one for an electric immersion heater and one for a thermometer (a drop of oil helps contact). Measure the block's mass. Record the starting temperature, switch on the heater, and use a joulemeter (or a voltmeter, ammeter and stopwatch: E = IVt) to find the energy supplied. Record the final temperature. Calculate c = ΔE ÷ (mΔθ). Wrap the block in insulation to reduce energy lost to the surroundings, which otherwise makes the value of c too high.\n\n**Supplement — measuring c of a liquid:** the same method, with the heater and thermometer in a known mass of liquid in an insulated beaker, stirring so the temperature is even.\n\n### Melting, boiling and evaporation — 2.2.3\n\nDuring melting and boiling, energy is supplied but the temperature stays constant until the change of state is complete. The energy is used to break the forces between the particles (it increases the internal energy without raising the average kinetic energy). On a heating graph these show as flat sections.\n\nAt standard atmospheric pressure, pure water melts at 0 °C and boils at 100 °C.\n\nCondensation: gas particles slow down as they cool, come close together and are held by attractive forces to form a liquid; energy is released. Solidification: liquid particles lose energy and settle into fixed positions in a regular arrangement.\n\nEvaporation is the escape of the more energetic particles from the surface of a liquid. The particles left behind have a lower average kinetic energy, so the liquid cools.\n\n**Supplement — boiling compared with evaporation:**\n\n| Boiling | Evaporation |\n|---|---|\n| Happens at one temperature (the boiling point) | Happens at any temperature |\n| Happens throughout the liquid (bubbles form) | Happens only at the surface |\n| Needs a continuous energy supply to continue | Can happen without heating; the liquid cools |\n\n**Supplement — what speeds up evaporation:** a higher temperature (more particles have enough energy to escape), a larger surface area (more particles are at the surface), and air movement over the surface (wind carries escaped particles away so fewer return). Wet clothes dry fastest on a warm, windy day, spread out.\n\n**Supplement — cooling by evaporation:** sweat on skin evaporates; the most energetic particles escape, the remaining liquid cools and takes energy from the skin, so the skin cools. Alcohol on the hand feels cold for the same reason.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| c = ΔE ÷ (mΔθ) | c: specific heat capacity, J/(kg °C); ΔE: energy supplied, J; m: mass, kg; Δθ: temperature change, °C | Heating or cooling with no change of state. Rearranged: ΔE = mcΔθ, Δθ = ΔE ÷ (mc), m = ΔE ÷ (cΔθ). | Supplement |\n| ΔE = Pt | P: heater power, W; t: time, s | Energy supplied by an electrical heater (from Unit 6). | Core |\n| ΔE = IVt | I: current, A; V: p.d., V; t: s | Energy supplied, measured with ammeter, voltmeter and timer (from Unit 15). | Core |\n| Δθ = final temperature − initial temperature | °C (same size as K) | Always a temperature change, never a single temperature. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: energy to heat something (Supplement)\n\n**Example — kettle.** How much energy heats 1.5 kg of water from 20 °C to 100 °C? (c = 4200 J/(kg °C))\n\n- Δθ = 100 − 20 = 80 °C.\n- ΔE = mcΔθ = 1.5 × 4200 × 80 = 504 000 J = 504 kJ.\n\n### Situation: find the temperature rise (Supplement)\n\n**Example — copper block.** 7800 J is supplied to a 0.50 kg copper block (c = 390 J/(kg °C)).\n\n- Δθ = ΔE ÷ (mc) = 7800 ÷ (0.50 × 390) = 40 °C.\n- If it started at 22 °C, it ends at 62 °C.\n\n### Situation: find c from an experiment (Supplement)\n\n**Example — heater and block.** A 50 W heater runs for 300 s in a 1.0 kg aluminium block. The temperature rises from 20 °C to 35 °C.\n\n- ΔE = Pt = 50 × 300 = 15 000 J.\n- c = 15 000 ÷ (1.0 × 15) = 1000 J/(kg °C).\n- The accepted value is about 900 J/(kg °C). The experimental value is high because some energy escaped to the surroundings, so the temperature rose less than it should.\n\n**Example — with meters.** A heater has 12 V across it and 4.0 A through it for 5 minutes, heating 2.0 kg of a liquid by 6.0 °C.\n\n- ΔE = IVt = 4.0 × 12 × 300 = 14 400 J.\n- c = 14 400 ÷ (2.0 × 6.0) = 1200 J/(kg °C).\n\n### Situation: find the time (Supplement)\n\n**Example — immersion heater.** How long does a 3000 W heater take to warm 100 kg of water by 40 °C, assuming no energy is lost?\n\n- ΔE = 100 × 4200 × 40 = 16 800 000 J.\n- t = ΔE ÷ P = 16 800 000 ÷ 3000 = 5600 s, about 93 minutes.\n\n### Situation: cooling (Supplement)\n\n**Example — energy released.** 0.20 kg of water cools from 80 °C to 30 °C. Energy released = 0.20 × 4200 × 50 = 42 000 J.\n\n### Situation: reading a heating curve\n\n**Example — interpreting the graph.** Ice at −10 °C is heated steadily. The graph rises to 0 °C, stays flat while the ice melts, rises to 100 °C, then stays flat while the water boils. On the flat parts energy is still going in, but it breaks the forces between particles instead of raising the temperature. The boiling section is longer than the melting section because separating particles completely takes much more energy than loosening them.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Δθ is a change in temperature: subtract. A change of 1 °C equals a change of 1 K.\n- Mass in kg in c = ΔE ÷ (mΔθ): 500 g = 0.50 kg.\n- A temperature that stops rising while energy is supplied means a change of state.\n- Evaporation cools because the most energetic particles leave, lowering the average kinetic energy of those left.\n- Measured values of c are usually too high because of energy lost to the surroundings; suggest insulation and a lid.\n- Expansion order: gases most, then liquids, then solids, explained by the forces between particles.\n",
  "igcse-u10": "# Unit 10 — Transfer of thermal energy\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 2.3.1, 2.3.2, 2.3.3 and 2.3.4, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Conduction — 2.3.1\n\nConduction is the transfer of thermal energy through a material without the material itself moving. It is the main way energy passes through solids.\n\nGood thermal conductors: metals (copper, aluminium, iron). Bad conductors (thermal insulators): plastic, wood, glass, air, wool, polystyrene.\n\n**Experiments:** attach drawing pins with wax along rods of different materials of the same size, and heat one end of each equally (for example, rods arranged around the edge of a heated container). The pins drop off first from the best conductor. Or wrap paper around a rod that is half wood and half brass and pass it through a flame: the paper over the wood scorches, but the paper over the brass does not, because the brass conducts the energy away. Or heat the top of a test tube of water with ice weighted at the bottom: the water at the top boils while the ice stays frozen, showing water is a poor conductor.\n\n**Supplement — how conduction works:** in all solids, the particles at the hot end vibrate more, and pass the vibration on to their neighbours through the bonds of the lattice (lattice vibration). In metals there is also a second, faster process: free (delocalised) electrons gain kinetic energy at the hot end, move quickly through the metal, and collide with particles further along, transferring energy. That is why metals are much better conductors than non-metals.\n\n**Supplement:** in gases and most liquids the particles are far apart (or not held in a lattice), so collisions that pass on energy are much less frequent. That makes them bad conductors. Many solids (such as glass or brick) conduct better than insulators but much less well than metals.\n\n### Convection — 2.3.2\n\nConvection is the main way thermal energy moves through liquids and gases. It cannot happen in solids, because the particles cannot flow.\n\nWhen part of a fluid is heated, it expands, so its density decreases and it rises. Cooler, denser fluid sinks to take its place and is heated in turn. This sets up a convection current.\n\n**Experiment:** drop a crystal of potassium permanganate into the bottom of a beaker of water near one edge, and heat gently below it. The purple colour rises above the heat, spreads across the top, and sinks down the far side, showing the convection current. For air, a candle under one chimney of a box draws smoke from a smouldering straw down the other chimney.\n\n### Thermal radiation — 2.3.3\n\nThermal radiation is infrared radiation, part of the electromagnetic spectrum. All objects emit it; hotter objects emit more. It needs no medium, so it can travel through a vacuum, which is how energy reaches us from the Sun.\n\nThe surface affects radiation:\n\n- Dull black surfaces are the best emitters and the best absorbers of infrared.\n- Shiny white or silver surfaces are the worst emitters and worst absorbers; they are the best reflectors.\n\n**Supplement:** an object stays at a constant temperature when it transfers energy away at the same rate as it receives energy. If it receives energy faster than it loses it, its temperature rises; if it loses energy faster, its temperature falls. The Earth's temperature depends on this balance between the incoming radiation from the Sun and the radiation the Earth's surface emits. Greenhouse gases (carbon dioxide, methane, water vapour) absorb and re-emit some of the infrared emitted by the surface, so less escapes to space and the average temperature rises; clouds, ice and snow reflect incoming radiation.\n\n**Supplement:** the rate at which an object emits radiation increases with its surface temperature and with its surface area. A large-fin radiator or a large-eared desert fox loses energy faster.\n\n**Supplement — emitters experiment (Leslie's cube):** a metal cube with four different faces (dull black, shiny black, dull white, shiny silver) is filled with hot water so all faces are at the same temperature. An infrared detector (or a thermometer with a blackened bulb) at the same distance from each face shows the dull black face emits most and the shiny silver face least.\n\n**Supplement — absorbers experiment:** place two identical metal plates, one dull black and one shiny, at equal distances from a radiant heater, with a drawing pin stuck to the back of each with wax. The wax behind the dull black plate melts first, so its pin drops first. Or use two thermometers, one with a blackened bulb, in sunlight.\n\n### Consequences of thermal energy transfer — 2.3.4\n\n- **Kitchen pan:** the base is metal, a good conductor, so energy passes quickly from the hob to the food. The handle is plastic or wood, an insulator, so it stays cool enough to hold.\n- **Heating a room:** a \"radiator\" works mainly by convection. Air next to it is heated, expands, becomes less dense and rises; cooler air sinks to replace it, circulating warm air around the room. Heaters are placed low down; a cooling unit in a fridge is placed at the top.\n- **Vacuum flask:** the vacuum stops conduction and convection; silvered walls reduce radiation; the stopper reduces convection and evaporation.\n- **Insulating a house:** loft insulation and cavity-wall foam trap air in small pockets, so it cannot move (no convection) and air is a poor conductor; double glazing has an air or vacuum gap.\n\n**Supplement — more than one process at once:**\n\n- **A fire burning wood or coal:** radiation heats people and objects in front of it (it travels in straight lines through air); convection carries hot gases and smoke up the chimney while fresh air is drawn in at the bottom, feeding the fire; conduction heats the grate and surrounding brickwork.\n- **A car radiator:** the hot engine heats the coolant by conduction; the coolant is pumped to the radiator, which has thin metal fins with a large surface area; energy is conducted through the metal, then carried away by air flowing past (convection, helped by the fan and the car's motion), and some is radiated.\n\n## Key formulas\n\nThere are no equations to recall in this unit. These relationships replace them:\n\n| Relationship | Meaning | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Temperature constant when energy in per second = energy out per second | Thermal equilibrium | Explaining a steady temperature, the Earth's temperature, why an object warms or cools | Supplement |\n| Rate of emission increases with surface temperature and surface area | Hotter and larger radiate faster | Cooling fins, radiators, animals' ears | Supplement |\n| Dull black: best emitter and absorber; shiny silver or white: worst emitter and absorber, best reflector | Effect of surface on infrared | Solar panels (black), fire suits and survival blankets (shiny), houses in hot countries (white) | Core |\n| Heated fluid: expands → density falls → rises | Cause of convection | Any convection explanation | Core |\n\n## Using the ideas in different situations\n\n### Situation: choose a material\n\n**Example — saucepan.** Copper base: a good conductor, so energy reaches the food quickly. Plastic handle: a poor conductor, so it stays cool.\n\n**Example — winter clothing.** Several thin layers trap air, a poor conductor; the trapped air cannot move, so convection is reduced.\n\n### Situation: explain with convection\n\n**Example — sea breeze.** By day the land heats up faster than the sea. Air over the land is heated, expands, becomes less dense and rises; cooler air from over the sea moves in to replace it.\n\n**Example — where to put a heater.** At the bottom of a room, so the warm, less dense air rises and circulates. A freezer compartment goes at the top of a fridge, so cooled, denser air sinks.\n\n### Situation: explain with radiation\n\n**Example — solar water panel.** The pipes are painted dull black so they absorb as much infrared as possible.\n\n**Example — teapot or survival blanket.** A shiny surface emits little infrared, so the contents or the person stay warm longer.\n\n**Example — houses in hot countries** are painted white to reflect infrared, so they absorb less and stay cooler.\n\n### Situation: energy balance (Supplement)\n\n**Example — car parked in the sun.** In the morning, the car receives more energy from the Sun per second than it emits, so it warms up. As it gets hotter, it emits more radiation per second, until emission equals absorption and its temperature stops rising.\n\n**Example — Earth.** If greenhouse gases increase, less infrared escapes per second than the Earth receives, so the Earth warms until it emits enough to balance again, at a higher temperature.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Conduction: solids (lattice vibrations; free electrons in metals). Convection: liquids and gases only. Radiation: needs no medium.\n- Explain convection with expand → less dense → rises; never say \"heat rises\".\n- Dull black is best at both emitting and absorbing. Shiny is worst at both, best at reflecting.\n- Thermal radiation is infrared, an electromagnetic wave.\n- Trapped air insulates because it is a poor conductor and it cannot move to form convection currents.\n- A constant temperature means the rates of energy in and out are equal, not that no energy is moving.\n",
  "igcse-u11": "# Unit 11 — General properties of waves\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 3.1, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### What a wave is — 3.1\n\nWaves transfer energy from one place to another without transferring matter. When a wave passes along a rope, each part of the rope vibrates about a fixed position; the rope itself does not travel along. A cork on a pond bobs up and down as ripples pass; it is not carried to the shore.\n\nWave motion can be shown with a rope or a slinky spring (shake one end), and with water waves in a ripple tank.\n\n### Describing a wave — 3.1\n\n- **Crest (peak):** the highest point of a transverse wave. **Trough:** the lowest point.\n- **Amplitude:** the maximum displacement from the rest (undisturbed) position: from the middle to a crest, not crest to trough.\n- **Wavelength, λ:** the distance between two neighbouring identical points on the wave, such as crest to crest. Unit: m.\n- **Frequency, f:** the number of complete waves passing a point per second. Unit: hertz (Hz); 1 Hz = 1 wave per second.\n- **Wave speed, v:** the distance a wave travels per second. Unit: m/s.\n- **Wavefront:** a line joining points on the same part of a wave, such as all of one crest. In a ripple tank, wavefronts are the bright lines; the distance between neighbouring wavefronts is one wavelength.\n- **Period, T:** the time for one complete wave to pass a point. T = 1 ÷ f.\n\n### Transverse and longitudinal waves — 3.1\n\n**Transverse:** the vibrations are at right angles to the direction the wave travels (propagates). Examples: all electromagnetic waves (light, radio, X-rays), water waves, seismic S-waves (secondary).\n\n**Longitudinal:** the vibrations are parallel to the direction the wave travels, producing compressions (particles close together) and rarefactions (particles spread out). Examples: sound waves, seismic P-waves (primary). A slinky pushed in and out along its length shows a longitudinal wave; shaken side to side, it shows a transverse wave.\n\n### Reflection, refraction and diffraction — 3.1\n\n- **Reflection at a plane surface:** the wave bounces off. The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection; wavelength, frequency and speed are unchanged.\n- **Refraction:** a change of direction when a wave changes speed as it enters a different medium (or, for water waves, a different depth), arriving at an angle. Water waves slow down in shallow water, so their wavelength gets shorter; the frequency stays the same, because it is set by the source.\n- **Diffraction:** waves spread out as they pass through a gap or past an edge. Wavelength, frequency and speed are unchanged.\n\n**Ripple tank demonstrations:** a straight vibrating bar makes plane (straight) waves. Place a flat barrier at an angle to show reflection. Place a glass plate on the bottom to make a shallow region: the wavefronts get closer together (shorter wavelength, slower) and bend at an angled boundary. Place two barriers with a gap to show diffraction through a gap, or one barrier to show diffraction at an edge. A lamp above the tank projects the wavefronts onto paper below.\n\n**Supplement — how much diffraction:**\n\n- Through a gap: diffraction is greatest when the gap is about the same size as the wavelength. A gap much wider than the wavelength gives only slight spreading at the edges. For a fixed gap, a longer wavelength diffracts more.\n- At an edge: longer wavelengths bend further round the edge than shorter ones.\n\nThis is why you can hear (long wavelength sound) round a corner but not see (very short wavelength light) round it, and why long-wave radio signals reach valleys that short-wave signals do not.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| v = fλ | v: wave speed, m/s; f: frequency, Hz; λ (lambda): wavelength, m | Every type of wave. Rearranged: f = v ÷ λ, λ = v ÷ f. | Core |\n| v = distance ÷ time | m/s | Speed from how far a wavefront moves in a measured time. | Core |\n| f = number of waves ÷ time | Hz | Counting waves passing a point. | Core |\n| T = 1 ÷ f | T: period, s; f: Hz | Time for one wave, for example from an oscilloscope trace. Useful, not a listed syllabus equation. | Core |\n\nPrefixes: kHz = 10³ Hz, MHz = 10⁶ Hz, GHz = 10⁹ Hz; mm = 10⁻³ m, nm = 10⁻⁹ m.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: find the speed\n\n**Example — water wave.** Waves of frequency 4.0 Hz and wavelength 0.15 m. v = fλ = 4.0 × 0.15 = 0.60 m/s.\n\n### Situation: find the wavelength\n\n**Example — radio.** A radio station broadcasts at 100 MHz; radio waves travel at 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.\n\n- λ = v ÷ f = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ (100 × 10⁶) = 3.0 m.\n\n**Example — sound.** A 440 Hz note in air (speed 330 m/s): λ = 330 ÷ 440 = 0.75 m.\n\n### Situation: find the frequency\n\n**Example — light.** Red light has a wavelength of 7.0 × 10⁻⁷ m. f = v ÷ λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ (7.0 × 10⁻⁷) = 4.3 × 10¹⁴ Hz.\n\n### Situation: information from a diagram\n\n**Example — counting waves.** A diagram shows 3 complete waves over a distance of 1.2 m, and 6 waves pass a point every 2.0 s.\n\n- λ = 1.2 ÷ 3 = 0.40 m.\n- f = 6 ÷ 2.0 = 3.0 Hz.\n- v = 3.0 × 0.40 = 1.2 m/s.\n\n**Example — reading the amplitude.** A transverse wave drawing has crests 8 cm above troughs. The amplitude is half the crest-to-trough height: 4 cm.\n\n### Situation: refraction into shallow water\n\n**Example — slower, same frequency.** Waves of frequency 5.0 Hz travel at 0.30 m/s in deep water and 0.20 m/s in shallow water.\n\n- In deep water: λ = 0.30 ÷ 5.0 = 0.060 m = 6.0 cm.\n- In shallow water: λ = 0.20 ÷ 5.0 = 0.040 m = 4.0 cm. The frequency stays 5.0 Hz.\n\n### Situation: wavefronts in a ripple tank\n\n**Example — from the spacing.** Ten wavefronts cover 12 cm on the screen and the vibrator runs at 8.0 Hz. The ten wavefronts span nine gaps, so λ = 12 ÷ 9 = 1.33 cm = 0.0133 m; v = 8.0 × 0.0133 = 0.11 m/s.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Waves transfer energy, not matter.\n- Amplitude is measured from the middle (rest position), not crest to trough.\n- Convert prefixes before using v = fλ (MHz to Hz, cm to m).\n- In refraction the frequency is unchanged; speed and wavelength change together.\n- Diffraction is most noticeable when the gap is similar in size to the wavelength.\n- Transverse: vibration at 90° to travel. Longitudinal: vibration along the direction of travel, with compressions and rarefactions.\n",
  "igcse-u12": "# Unit 12 — Light\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 3.2.1, 3.2.2, 3.2.3 and 3.2.4, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Reflection — 3.2.1\n\nThe normal is a line drawn at 90° to a surface at the point where a ray hits it. The angle of incidence, i, is between the incident ray and the normal; the angle of reflection, r, is between the reflected ray and the normal. Angles are always measured from the normal, not from the surface.\n\nLaw of reflection: the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection.\n\nThe image in a plane mirror is:\n\n- the same size as the object;\n- the same distance behind the mirror as the object is in front;\n- virtual (it cannot be projected onto a screen; the rays only appear to come from it);\n- upright, and laterally inverted (left and right swapped).\n\n**Supplement — constructions:** to locate the image, draw a line from the object at 90° to the mirror and continue it the same distance behind. To draw a ray reaching an eye, join the image to the eye with a straight line; where it crosses the mirror is where the real ray reflects; draw the ray from the object to that point, then on to the eye. Use solid lines for real rays and dashed lines behind the mirror.\n\n### Refraction — 3.2.2\n\nRefraction is the change in direction of light as it crosses a boundary between two media at an angle, because its speed changes. Light slows down in glass or water compared with air.\n\n- Entering a denser medium (air → glass): light slows and bends towards the normal.\n- Leaving a denser medium (glass → air): light speeds up and bends away from the normal.\n- A ray along the normal (i = 0) does not change direction, though its speed still changes.\n\n**Experiment:** place a rectangular glass block on paper and draw round it. Shine a ray from a ray box into one side at an angle; mark the ray with crosses where it enters and leaves. Remove the block and join the points to show the path inside. Measure i and r from the normal. Repeat for different angles and with semicircular or triangular blocks. A rectangular block gives an emerging ray parallel to the incident ray, but shifted sideways.\n\n### Critical angle and total internal reflection — 3.2.2\n\nWhen light travels from a denser medium towards a less dense one (glass → air), some is reflected internally and some refracts out. As the angle of incidence increases, the refracted ray bends closer to the surface. The critical angle, c, is the angle of incidence for which the angle of refraction is 90°.\n\nIf the angle of incidence is greater than the critical angle, all the light is reflected back inside: total internal reflection. Conditions: light must travel from the denser medium to the less dense one, and i must be greater than c. For glass c is about 42°.\n\nExamples: a semicircular block with a ray aimed at its centre (increase the angle until the ray reflects totally); periscopes and binoculars using 45° prisms; the sparkle of a cut diamond; optical fibres.\n\n**Supplement — refractive index:** n is the ratio of the speed of light in two regions: the speed in air (or a vacuum) divided by the speed in the material. Glass has n ≈ 1.5, so light travels 1.5 times slower in glass. A larger n means more bending.\n\n**Supplement — optical fibres:** thin, flexible glass fibres. Light entering one end hits the inside wall at more than the critical angle and is totally internally reflected again and again along the fibre, even round bends. Uses: telecommunications (telephone, cable TV and broadband, carrying data as pulses of light or infrared at high rates over long distances with little loss) and endoscopes for seeing inside the body.\n\n### Thin lenses — 3.2.3\n\nA converging (convex) lens is thicker in the middle and makes a parallel beam of light meet at a point: the principal focus (focal point). A diverging (concave) lens is thinner in the middle and makes a parallel beam spread out, as if from a principal focus behind the lens.\n\n- **Principal axis:** the line through the centre of the lens at 90° to it.\n- **Focal length, f:** the distance from the centre of the lens to the principal focus. A fatter (more curved) lens has a shorter focal length.\n\n**Ray diagram for a converging lens (real image):** use two of these three rays from the top of the object.\n\n- A ray parallel to the principal axis refracts through the principal focus on the far side.\n- A ray through the centre of the lens goes straight on.\n- A ray through the principal focus on the near side emerges parallel to the axis.\n\nWhere the rays cross is the top of the image. A real image is formed where rays actually meet; it can be shown on a screen.\n\n| Object position | Image |\n|---|---|\n| Further than 2f | Real, inverted, diminished, between f and 2f on the other side (camera, eye) |\n| At 2f | Real, inverted, same size, at 2f |\n| Between f and 2f | Real, inverted, enlarged, beyond 2f (projector) |\n| At f | No image: the rays emerge parallel |\n| Closer than f (Supplement) | Virtual, upright, enlarged, on the same side as the object (magnifying glass) |\n\nDescribe an image with three terms: enlarged / same size / diminished; upright / inverted; real / virtual.\n\nA virtual image forms where diverging rays appear to come from when traced backwards (drawn dashed). It cannot be projected onto a screen.\n\n**Supplement — virtual image ray diagram:** with the object closer than f, the two rays leave the lens diverging. Extend them backwards with dashed lines; they meet on the object's side, giving an upright, enlarged, virtual image. This is a magnifying glass: hold a converging lens so the object is inside its focal length and look through the lens.\n\n**Supplement — correcting sight:**\n\n- **Short sight:** distant objects are blurred because the eye focuses their light in front of the retina (the eye lens is too strong or the eyeball too long). Corrected with a diverging lens, which spreads the rays slightly before they enter the eye.\n- **Long sight:** near objects are blurred because the light would focus behind the retina (the eye lens is too weak). Corrected with a converging lens, which starts converging the rays before they enter the eye.\n\n### Dispersion — 3.2.4\n\nWhite light passing through a glass prism splits into a spectrum of colours: dispersion. Each colour has a different frequency and slows by a different amount in glass, so each is refracted by a different angle. Violet is refracted most, red least.\n\nThe seven colours in order of increasing wavelength (decreasing frequency): violet, indigo, blue, green, yellow, orange, red. Red has the longest wavelength and lowest frequency; violet has the shortest wavelength and highest frequency.\n\n**Supplement:** light of a single frequency is monochromatic (a single colour); a laser gives monochromatic light.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| i = r (reflection) | i: angle of incidence; r: angle of reflection; both from the normal, degrees | Any plane-mirror question. | Core |\n| image distance behind mirror = object distance in front | m or cm | Locating plane-mirror images. | Core |\n| n = sin i ÷ sin r | n: refractive index, no unit; i: angle in air; r: angle in the material | Light passing between air and a material. Rearranged: sin r = sin i ÷ n. | Supplement |\n| n = speed in air ÷ speed in material | speeds in m/s | The definition of n; speed of light in air ≈ 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s. | Supplement |\n| n = 1 ÷ sin c | c: critical angle, degrees | Total internal reflection. Rearranged: sin c = 1 ÷ n. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: plane mirror\n\n**Example — angle between rays.** A ray hits a mirror at 30° to the surface. Its angle of incidence is 90 − 30 = 60°, so the angle of reflection is 60°. The angle between the incident and reflected rays is 120°.\n\n**Example — image position.** A person stands 1.5 m in front of a mirror. The image is 1.5 m behind the mirror, so 3.0 m from the person. If they step 0.5 m closer, the image is 2.0 m from them.\n\n### Situation: find the refractive index (Supplement)\n\n**Example — glass block.** A ray enters glass with i = 40° and r = 25°. n = sin 40° ÷ sin 25° = 0.643 ÷ 0.423 = 1.52.\n\n### Situation: find the angle of refraction (Supplement)\n\n**Example — water.** Light enters water (n = 1.33) at i = 50°. sin r = sin 50° ÷ 1.33 = 0.766 ÷ 1.33 = 0.576, so r = 35°.\n\n### Situation: find the speed in the material (Supplement)\n\n**Example — diamond.** n = 2.4. Speed in diamond = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ 2.4 = 1.25 × 10⁸ m/s.\n\n### Situation: critical angle (Supplement)\n\n**Example — find c.** For glass with n = 1.5: sin c = 1 ÷ 1.5 = 0.667, so c = 42°.\n\n**Example — find n.** A block has a critical angle of 37°. n = 1 ÷ sin 37° = 1 ÷ 0.602 = 1.66.\n\n**Example — does it reflect totally?** Light inside glass (c = 42°) meets the surface at 45°. Since 45° is greater than 42° and the light is going from glass to air, it is totally internally reflected. At 30° it would mostly refract out into the air.\n\n### Situation: lens images\n\n**Example — camera.** An object is 30 cm from a converging lens of focal length 10 cm. It is beyond 2f (20 cm), so the image is real, inverted and diminished, between 10 cm and 20 cm on the other side (a scale drawing gives 15 cm).\n\n**Example — magnifying glass (Supplement).** An object 4 cm from a lens of focal length 6 cm is inside f, so the image is virtual, upright and enlarged, on the same side as the object.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Measure every angle from the normal.\n- Plane-mirror images are virtual, upright, the same size and the same distance behind the mirror.\n- Towards the normal when slowing down (into glass), away from the normal when speeding up (into air).\n- Total internal reflection needs both conditions: dense to less dense, and i greater than c.\n- In n = sin i ÷ sin r, i is the angle in air. Use your calculator in degree mode.\n- Draw rays with arrows and a ruler; virtual rays dashed.\n- Spectrum order by wavelength: violet (shortest) to red (longest).\n",
  "igcse-u13": "# Unit 13 — Electromagnetic spectrum and sound\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 3.3 and 3.4, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### The electromagnetic spectrum — 3.3\n\nElectromagnetic waves are transverse waves that all travel at the same high speed in a vacuum. **Supplement:** that speed is 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s, and it is approximately the same in air.\n\nIn order of increasing frequency (decreasing wavelength):\n\n| Region | Typical uses | Harmful effects of excessive exposure |\n|---|---|---|\n| Radio waves (longest wavelength, lowest frequency) | Radio and television transmissions, astronomy, radio frequency identification (RFID) tags | — |\n| Microwaves | Satellite television, mobile phones, microwave ovens | Internal heating of body cells |\n| Infrared | Electric grills, remote controls for TVs, intruder alarms, thermal imaging, optical fibres | Skin burns |\n| Visible light | Vision, photography, illumination | — |\n| Ultraviolet | Security marking (seen under UV lamps), detecting fake bank notes, sterilising water | Damage to surface cells and eyes, leading to skin cancer and eye conditions |\n| X-rays | Medical scanning (bones), security scanners at airports | Mutation or damage to cells in the body |\n| Gamma rays (shortest wavelength, highest frequency) | Sterilising food and medical equipment, detecting and treating cancer | Mutation or damage to cells in the body |\n\nA memory aid for the order: Radio, Micro, Infrared, Visible, Ultraviolet, X, Gamma. Higher frequency means more energy carried and generally more danger.\n\n### Communication with satellites — 3.3\n\nCommunication with artificial satellites is mainly by microwaves, because they pass through the atmosphere.\n\n- Some satellite phones use satellites in low orbits (close to Earth, so a weaker signal is needed, but each satellite moves across the sky, so many are needed).\n- Some satellite phones and direct-broadcast satellite TV use geostationary satellites, which stay above the same point on the equator, so a dish can point at one fixed position.\n\n### Communication systems — 3.3 (Supplement)\n\n- **Mobile phones and wireless internet** use microwaves: they can pass through some walls and need only a short aerial to send and receive.\n- **Bluetooth** uses radio waves: they pass through walls, though the signal is weakened.\n- **Optical fibres** (visible light or short-wavelength infrared) carry cable TV and high-speed broadband: glass is transparent to these wavelengths, and their high frequency can carry data at high rates.\n\n### Analogue and digital signals — 3.3 (Supplement)\n\nAn analogue signal varies continuously and can take any value. A digital signal has only two values, on and off (1 and 0). Sound can be sent either way: a microphone produces an analogue signal, which can be converted to digital.\n\nBenefits of digital signals:\n\n- a higher rate of data transmission;\n- a longer range, because at each regenerator (amplifier) along the way the signal can be cleaned up exactly: noise picked up on the way is removed, since each pulse only needs to be recognised as a 1 or a 0. Amplifying an analogue signal amplifies its noise too.\n\n### Sound — 3.4\n\nSound is produced by vibrating sources (a guitar string, a loudspeaker cone, vocal cords). The vibrations pass through a medium as a longitudinal wave: the particles vibrate backwards and forwards along the direction the wave travels.\n\nSound needs a medium (solid, liquid or gas); it cannot travel through a vacuum. Shown by the bell-in-a-jar experiment: as air is pumped out, the ringing fades, though you can still see the hammer striking.\n\n- Humans hear frequencies from about 20 Hz to 20 000 Hz.\n- Ultrasound is sound with a frequency higher than 20 kHz (20 000 Hz).\n- The speed of sound in air is about 330 to 350 m/s. **Supplement:** sound generally travels faster in solids than in liquids, and faster in liquids than in gases (about 1500 m/s in water, about 5000 m/s in steel).\n- An echo is the reflection of a sound wave off a hard surface.\n\nLoudness depends on amplitude: a larger amplitude sounds louder. Pitch depends on frequency: a higher frequency sounds higher.\n\n**Supplement — compressions and rarefactions:** a compression is a region where the particles are closer together (higher pressure); a rarefaction is a region where they are further apart (lower pressure). The wavelength is the distance from one compression to the next.\n\n### Measuring the speed of sound in air — 3.4\n\n**Echo method:** stand a measured distance (for example 100 m) from a large flat wall. Clap and adjust your clapping rate until each clap coincides with the echo of the one before. Time 20 claps; the sound travels to the wall and back (2 × 100 m) in each clap interval. Speed = 2 × distance ÷ time for one interval.\n\n**Two-observer method:** one person fires a starting pistol (or bangs cymbals) a measured distance away (several hundred metres). The other starts a stopwatch on seeing the flash (light arrives almost instantly) and stops it on hearing the bang. Speed = distance ÷ time. Repeat and average; swap positions to cancel the effect of wind.\n\n### Uses of ultrasound — 3.4 (Supplement)\n\nUltrasound pulses reflect from boundaries between different materials. The time for the echo to return gives the distance.\n\n- **Non-destructive testing:** detecting cracks inside metal castings or welds; an echo returns early from a crack.\n- **Medical scanning of soft tissue:** imaging a fetus or organs; it is safe because it is not ionising.\n- **Sonar:** finding the depth of the sea or locating shoals of fish and submarines.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| v = fλ | v: speed, m/s; f: frequency, Hz; λ: wavelength, m | Any electromagnetic or sound wave. | Core |\n| v = d ÷ t | d: distance travelled by the sound, m; t: time, s | Speed of sound by the two-observer method; any wave over a known distance. | Core |\n| distance to reflector = (v × t) ÷ 2 | t: time from sending to receiving the echo, s | Echoes, sonar and ultrasound: the sound goes there and back. | Supplement |\n| speed of EM waves in a vacuum = 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s | m/s | All EM wave calculations; the same value in air. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: electromagnetic waves\n\n**Example — microwaves.** A microwave oven uses 2.45 GHz. λ = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ (2.45 × 10⁹) = 0.12 m.\n\n**Example — X-rays.** An X-ray has wavelength 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. f = 3.0 × 10⁸ ÷ (1.0 × 10⁻¹⁰) = 3.0 × 10¹⁸ Hz.\n\n### Situation: sound wavelength and frequency\n\n**Example — find the wavelength.** A 256 Hz tuning fork in air (340 m/s): λ = 340 ÷ 256 = 1.3 m.\n\n**Example — ultrasound.** 40 kHz ultrasound in air (340 m/s): λ = 340 ÷ 40 000 = 0.0085 m = 8.5 mm.\n\n### Situation: two-observer speed of sound\n\n**Example.** A starting pistol is fired 660 m away; the bang is heard 2.0 s after the flash is seen. v = 660 ÷ 2.0 = 330 m/s.\n\n### Situation: echo off a wall\n\n**Example — find the speed.** A student 85 m from a wall hears the echo of a clap 0.50 s later. The sound travels 2 × 85 = 170 m. v = 170 ÷ 0.50 = 340 m/s.\n\n**Example — find the distance.** An echo returns 1.2 s after a shout; v = 340 m/s. Distance to the cliff = (340 × 1.2) ÷ 2 = 204 m.\n\n### Situation: sonar and ultrasound (Supplement)\n\n**Example — depth of the sea.** A ship's sonar pulse returns after 0.80 s; sound travels at 1500 m/s in sea water. Depth = (1500 × 0.80) ÷ 2 = 600 m.\n\n**Example — crack in metal.** Ultrasound in steel (5000 m/s). The echo from the far side of a block returns after 40 µs, but another echo returns after 16 µs.\n\n- Thickness = (5000 × 40 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ 2 = 0.10 m.\n- Crack depth = (5000 × 16 × 10⁻⁶) ÷ 2 = 0.040 m = 4.0 cm below the surface.\n\n### Situation: thunder and lightning\n\n**Example.** Thunder is heard 3.0 s after the lightning flash; v = 340 m/s. Distance = 340 × 3.0 = 1020 m, about 1 km. (No halving: the sound only travels one way.)\n\n### Situation: comparing sounds\n\n**Example — reading an oscilloscope.** Trace B has taller peaks than trace A but the same spacing: B is louder, same pitch. Trace C has peaks closer together than A: C has a higher frequency, so a higher pitch.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Learn the EM spectrum order both ways, and one use and one hazard for each region.\n- Sound cannot travel through a vacuum; light can.\n- In echo questions, halve the total distance (there and back) — but not in one-way questions such as thunder.\n- Loudness ↔ amplitude; pitch ↔ frequency.\n- Ultrasound is above 20 kHz; convert kHz to Hz before calculating.\n- Speeds: light 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s; sound in air about 330 to 350 m/s; sound is faster in liquids and faster still in solids.\n",
  "igcse-u14": "# Unit 14 — Magnetism\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 4.1, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Magnetic poles and forces — 4.1\n\nEvery magnet has a north (N) pole and a south (S) pole, where the magnetic effect is strongest.\n\n- Like poles repel: N–N or S–S push apart.\n- Unlike poles attract: N–S pull together.\n- A magnet attracts unmagnetised magnetic materials (iron, steel, nickel, cobalt), whichever pole is used.\n\nRepulsion is the only sure test that an object is a magnet: an unmagnetised piece of iron is attracted by both poles, but a magnet is repelled by one of them.\n\nA material is **magnetised** when it behaves as a magnet, and **unmagnetised** when it does not. **Magnetic materials** are attracted by magnets and can be magnetised (iron, steel, nickel, cobalt). **Non-magnetic materials** are not attracted (copper, aluminium, wood, plastic, glass).\n\n### Induced magnetism — 4.1\n\nA magnetic material placed near or touching a magnet becomes a magnet itself: its magnetism is induced. The end nearest the magnet's N pole becomes an S pole, which is why it is attracted. A chain of paper clips can hang from a magnet because each clip induces magnetism in the next.\n\n### Temporary and permanent magnets — 4.1\n\n| Soft iron | Steel |\n|---|---|\n| Easy to magnetise | Harder to magnetise |\n| Loses its magnetism easily when removed from the field (temporary magnet) | Keeps its magnetism (permanent magnet) |\n| Used in electromagnets, transformer cores, relays | Used in compasses, fridge magnets, loudspeakers, motors |\n\n### Magnetic fields — 4.1\n\nA magnetic field is a region in which a magnetic pole experiences a force. The direction of the field at a point is the direction of the force on a north pole placed there. So field lines point away from N poles and towards S poles.\n\nField pattern of a bar magnet: lines come out of the N pole, curve round, and go into the S pole. They never cross. They are closest together near the poles, where the field is strongest.\n\nBetween two facing N poles the lines push away from each other, leaving a neutral point midway where there is no field. Between a facing N and S pole the lines run straight across, giving a nearly uniform field.\n\n**Plotting the field:**\n\n- **Plotting compass:** put the magnet on paper. Place a small compass near the N pole and mark the position the needle's N end points to. Move the compass so its tail is on the mark, mark again, and continue to the S pole. Join the dots to form one line, with an arrow; repeat from other starting points. The compass also shows the field's direction.\n- **Iron filings:** put paper over the magnet, sprinkle filings and tap gently. The filings line up along the field lines, showing the pattern quickly, but not the direction.\n\n### Uses of magnets — 4.1\n\n- **Permanent magnets:** compasses, fridge door seals, loudspeakers, microphones, electric motors and generators, magnetic catches.\n- **Electromagnets** (a coil of wire around a soft-iron core, magnetic only when current flows): scrap-yard cranes that can drop their load by switching off, relays, electric bells, magnetic door locks, MRI scanners. Their strength can be varied by changing the current.\n\n**Supplement:** magnetic forces are due to interactions between magnetic fields: the field of one magnet acts on the other. The relative strength of a field is shown by the spacing of the field lines: closer lines mean a stronger field.\n\n## Key ideas\n\nThere are no equations to recall in this unit. Use these rules:\n\n| Rule | Meaning | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Like poles repel, unlike poles attract | Forces between poles | Predicting which way magnets move | Core |\n| Field direction = direction of the force on a N pole | Defines the arrows on field lines | Drawing fields: N → S outside the magnet | Core |\n| Soft iron: temporary; steel: permanent | Choosing materials | Electromagnet cores vs compass needles | Core |\n| Closer field lines = stronger field | Field strength from a diagram | Comparing points on a field diagram | Supplement |\n\n## Using the ideas in different situations\n\n### Situation: is it a magnet?\n\n**Example — three bars.** Bar A repels one end of a known magnet; bar B is attracted by both ends; bar C is not affected. A is a magnet; B is an unmagnetised magnetic material (such as iron); C is non-magnetic (such as aluminium).\n\n### Situation: drawing field lines\n\n**Example — two magnets.** Two bar magnets lie end to end with N facing S. Draw lines running straight from the N pole across to the S pole in the gap, close together and evenly spaced (uniform, strong). With N facing N, the lines curve away from each other, and there is a neutral point in the middle with no lines.\n\n### Situation: choosing a material\n\n**Example — crane.** A scrap-yard crane uses an electromagnet with a soft-iron core: soft iron magnetises strongly when current flows and loses its magnetism when the current is switched off, releasing the scrap. Steel would stay magnetised and keep holding on.\n\n### Situation: induced magnetism\n\n**Example — which pole?** The N pole of a magnet is brought near the left end of an iron nail. The left end becomes an S pole and the right end an N pole, so the nail is attracted. A second nail touching the right end becomes magnetised in the same way.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Only repulsion proves an object is a magnet.\n- Arrows on field lines go from N to S outside the magnet.\n- Field lines never cross; they are densest at the poles.\n- Iron filings show the pattern; a compass shows the pattern and the direction.\n- Soft iron for temporary magnets (electromagnets, transformers); steel for permanent magnets.\n",
  "igcse-u15": "# Unit 15 — Electrical quantities\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 4.2.1, 4.2.2, 4.2.3, 4.2.4 and 4.2.5, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Electric charge — 4.2.1\n\nThere are two kinds of charge, positive and negative. Like charges repel; unlike charges attract.\n\nRubbing two insulators together (a polythene rod with a cloth) transfers electrons from one to the other. Only negative charges (electrons) move. The object that gains electrons becomes negatively charged; the one that loses them is left positively charged, with equal and opposite charge. Polythene gains electrons (negative); acetate loses electrons (positive).\n\n**Experiments:** rub a rod and hang it from a thread; bring another charged rod near: it is repelled (same charge) or attracted (opposite charge). Detect charge with a gold-leaf electroscope (the leaf rises when the cap is charged) or by the rod picking up small pieces of paper.\n\n**Conductors and insulators:** metals have free electrons that can move through them, so charge flows: they are conductors (copper, aluminium, graphite). In insulators the electrons are held tightly in place and cannot flow (plastic, rubber, glass, dry wood). To test, put the material in a gap in a circuit with a cell and a lamp: if the lamp lights, it is a conductor.\n\n**Supplement:** charge is measured in coulombs (C). An electric field is a region in which an electric charge experiences a force. The direction of the field at a point is the direction of the force on a positive charge there.\n\n**Supplement — field patterns:**\n\n- Around a positive point charge: straight lines radiating outwards. Negative point charge: straight lines pointing inwards.\n- Around a charged conducting sphere: the same radial pattern outside the sphere, at 90° to its surface.\n- Between two oppositely charged parallel plates: evenly spaced parallel lines from the positive plate to the negative plate (a uniform field), ignoring the edges.\n\n### Electric current — 4.2.2\n\nElectric current is a flow of charge. In metals the charge carriers are free electrons. Current is measured in amperes (A) with an ammeter connected in series. Analogue ammeters have a needle and scale; digital ammeters show a number. Choose a range just above the expected current for the most precise reading (for example a 0 to 1 A range for 0.35 A rather than 0 to 10 A).\n\nDirect current (d.c.) flows in one direction only (from a cell or battery). Alternating current (a.c.) keeps reversing direction (the mains supply, 50 Hz or 60 Hz depending on the country).\n\n**Supplement:** current is the charge passing a point per unit time. Conventional current flows from positive to negative around a circuit; the free electrons actually flow the other way, from negative to positive.\n\n### E.m.f. and potential difference — 4.2.3\n\n**Electromotive force (e.m.f.):** the electrical work done by a source in moving a unit charge around a complete circuit. Measured in volts (V). A 6 V battery gives 6 J to each coulomb of charge.\n\n**Potential difference (p.d.):** the work done by a unit charge passing through a component, which is the energy transferred per unit charge from electrical to other forms in that component. Measured in volts with a voltmeter connected in parallel across the component.\n\nE.m.f. is energy given to the charge by the source; p.d. is energy given up by the charge in a component. As with ammeters, choose the voltmeter range just above the expected reading.\n\n### Resistance — 4.2.4\n\nResistance opposes the current. R = V ÷ I; unit ohm (Ω). A larger resistance needs a larger p.d. to drive the same current.\n\n**Experiment:** connect the component in series with an ammeter, a variable resistor and a supply; connect a voltmeter in parallel across the component. Adjust the variable resistor to get several pairs of V and I readings; calculate R = V ÷ I for each, or plot I against V.\n\nFor a metal wire: a longer wire has a larger resistance; a thicker wire (larger cross-sectional area) has a smaller resistance. **Supplement:** resistance is directly proportional to length and inversely proportional to cross-sectional area. Double the length → double the resistance. Double the area → half the resistance.\n\n**Supplement — current–voltage (I–V) graphs:**\n\n- **Fixed resistor (at constant temperature):** a straight line through the origin. Current is proportional to p.d.; the resistance is constant.\n- **Filament lamp:** a curve that gets less steep as V increases. As the current increases, the filament gets hotter, its resistance increases, and the current rises less for each extra volt. The graph is the same shape for reversed p.d.\n- **Diode:** almost no current in the reverse direction (very high resistance); in the forward direction, current flows once the p.d. passes a small threshold (about 0.6 V), then rises steeply (low resistance). A diode lets current flow one way only.\n\n### Electrical energy and power — 4.2.5\n\nCircuits transfer energy from a source (a cell, battery or mains supply) to the components, and from them to the surroundings (light, heating, kinetic energy of a motor).\n\nPower P = IV. Energy E = IVt (= Pt).\n\n**The kilowatt-hour (kWh):** the energy transferred by a 1 kW appliance in 1 hour. 1 kWh = 1000 W × 3600 s = 3 600 000 J. Electricity companies charge per kWh (\"unit\").\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R = V ÷ I | R: resistance, Ω; V: p.d., V; I: current, A | Any component. Rearranged: V = IR, I = V ÷ R. | Core |\n| P = IV | P: power, W; I: A; V: V | Power of an appliance or component. | Core |\n| E = IVt | E: energy, J; t: time, s | Energy transferred in a time. Equivalent: E = Pt. | Core |\n| energy in kWh = power in kW × time in hours | kWh | Electricity bills. | Core |\n| cost = energy in kWh × price per kWh | | Cost of using an appliance. | Core |\n| I = Q ÷ t | I: current, A; Q: charge, C; t: time, s | Charge flowing in a time. Rearranged: Q = It. | Supplement |\n| E = W ÷ Q | E: e.m.f., V; W: work done by the source, J; Q: charge, C | Energy given to charge by a source. | Supplement |\n| V = W ÷ Q | V: p.d., V; W: energy transferred in the component, J | Energy given up by charge in a component. Rearranged: W = QV. | Supplement |\n| R ∝ L; R ∝ 1 ÷ A | L: length, m; A: cross-sectional area, m² | Comparing wires of the same metal. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: resistance from meter readings\n\n**Example — resistor.** A voltmeter reads 6.0 V and an ammeter reads 0.30 A. R = 6.0 ÷ 0.30 = 20 Ω.\n\n**Example — find the current.** A 12 V supply across a 48 Ω heater. I = 12 ÷ 48 = 0.25 A.\n\n**Example — find the p.d.** 0.50 A flows through a 220 Ω resistor. V = IR = 0.50 × 220 = 110 V.\n\n**Example — mA.** A p.d. of 3.0 V drives 15 mA through an LED circuit resistor. I = 0.015 A, R = 3.0 ÷ 0.015 = 200 Ω.\n\n### Situation: charge and current (Supplement)\n\n**Example — charge.** A current of 2.0 A flows for 3 minutes. Q = It = 2.0 × 180 = 360 C.\n\n**Example — time.** How long for 50 C to pass with a current of 0.25 A? t = Q ÷ I = 50 ÷ 0.25 = 200 s.\n\n### Situation: energy per coulomb (Supplement)\n\n**Example — e.m.f.** A battery does 540 J of work moving 45 C round a circuit. E = 540 ÷ 45 = 12 V.\n\n**Example — p.d.** 30 C passes through a lamp with a p.d. of 6.0 V across it. Energy transferred W = QV = 30 × 6.0 = 180 J.\n\n### Situation: power\n\n**Example — kettle.** A kettle takes 10 A from a 230 V supply. P = 10 × 230 = 2300 W = 2.3 kW.\n\n**Example — find the current.** A 60 W lamp on 230 V: I = P ÷ V = 60 ÷ 230 = 0.26 A.\n\n### Situation: energy in joules\n\n**Example — heater.** A 12 V, 2.0 A heater runs for 5 minutes. E = IVt = 2.0 × 12 × 300 = 7200 J.\n\n### Situation: cost of electricity\n\n**Example — one appliance.** A 2.5 kW heater is used for 4 hours a day for 30 days, at 15 cents per kWh.\n\n- Energy = 2.5 × 4 × 30 = 300 kWh.\n- Cost = 300 × 15 = 4500 cents = 45.00 dollars.\n\n**Example — watts and minutes.** A 800 W microwave runs for 45 minutes. Energy = 0.8 kW × 0.75 h = 0.6 kWh. At 20 cents per kWh, cost = 12 cents.\n\n**Example — kWh to joules.** 0.6 kWh = 0.6 × 3 600 000 = 2 160 000 J.\n\n### Situation: wire dimensions (Supplement)\n\n**Example — longer and thicker.** A wire has resistance 8.0 Ω. A wire of the same metal, twice as long and with twice the cross-sectional area: × 2 for length, ÷ 2 for area, so still 8.0 Ω.\n\n**Example — half the diameter.** Halving the diameter makes the area a quarter (area ∝ diameter²), so the resistance becomes 4 times larger: 32 Ω.\n\n### Situation: reading I–V graphs (Supplement)\n\n**Example — lamp.** From an I–V graph a lamp carries 0.20 A at 2.0 V and 0.30 A at 6.0 V. R = 2.0 ÷ 0.20 = 10 Ω at first and 6.0 ÷ 0.30 = 20 Ω later: the resistance increases as the filament heats up.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Ammeter in series, voltmeter in parallel.\n- Convert mA to A (÷ 1000), kΩ to Ω (× 1000), minutes to s (× 60).\n- For cost, use kW and hours; for joules, use W and seconds.\n- Charging by friction moves electrons only.\n- Conventional current: + to −. Electron flow: − to +.\n- Filament lamp: resistance increases with temperature. Diode: current one way only.\n- In R ∝ 1 ÷ A, the area depends on the diameter squared.\n",
  "igcse-u16": "# Unit 16 — Electric circuits\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 4.3.1, 4.3.2 and 4.3.3, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Circuit components — 4.3.1\n\nYou need to draw and recognise the symbols for, and know how these behave: cells and batteries, power supplies (d.c. and a.c.), generators, potential dividers, switches, fixed and variable resistors, heaters, thermistors, light-dependent resistors, lamps, motors, bells, ammeters, voltmeters, magnetising coils, transformers, fuses and relays.\n\n- **Cell / battery:** a source of e.m.f.; a battery is two or more cells joined together. The longer line of a cell symbol is the positive terminal.\n- **Fixed resistor:** limits the current. **Variable resistor (rheostat):** its resistance can be changed to control the current, for example to dim a lamp.\n- **Thermistor (NTC):** its resistance decreases as its temperature increases. Used in temperature sensors, fire alarms and thermostats.\n- **Light-dependent resistor (LDR):** its resistance decreases as the light falling on it gets brighter. Used in automatic street lights and light meters.\n- **Fuse:** a thin wire that melts if the current gets too large, breaking the circuit.\n- **Relay:** a switch operated by an electromagnet, so a small current in one circuit can switch a large current in another.\n- **Heater, lamp, motor, bell:** transfer electrical energy to internal energy, light, kinetic energy, sound.\n\n**Supplement:** a **diode** lets current flow in one direction only (the direction of the arrow in its symbol). A **light-emitting diode (LED)** is a diode that gives out light when current flows forwards through it; it uses little energy. Diodes are used to change a.c. into d.c. (rectification) and to protect circuits from a battery connected the wrong way round.\n\n### Series circuits — 4.3.2\n\nComponents are connected one after another in a single loop.\n\n- The current is the same at every point.\n- The e.m.f.s of cells in series add up (if they all face the same way). A cell connected the wrong way round subtracts.\n- The combined resistance is the sum of the individual resistances.\n- **Supplement:** the p.d. of the source is shared between the components: the sum of the p.d.s across them equals the total. The largest resistance gets the largest share.\n\nIf one component breaks, the whole circuit stops.\n\n### Parallel circuits — 4.3.2\n\nComponents are connected in separate branches between the same two points.\n\n- The current from the source is larger than the current in any one branch: it splits between the branches and recombines.\n- The combined resistance of two resistors in parallel is less than either resistor on its own (there are more paths for the current).\n- **Supplement:** at a junction, the total current in equals the total current out. This is because charge is conserved: it is not created or destroyed, so the charge flowing in each second must flow out.\n- **Supplement:** the p.d. across each branch is the same, and equals the p.d. across the whole parallel arrangement.\n\n**Lamps in parallel (house lighting):** each lamp gets the full supply p.d., so it is at full brightness; each can be switched on and off independently; if one fails, the others stay on.\n\n### Potential dividers — 4.3.3\n\nFor a constant current, the p.d. across a conductor increases as its resistance increases (V = IR). Two resistors in series across a supply share the p.d. in the ratio of their resistances.\n\n**Supplement:** a variable potential divider is either a variable resistor or a sliding contact on a resistor track (a potentiometer) used to give an output p.d. that can be changed smoothly from zero up to the full supply. Replacing one resistor with a thermistor or LDR makes a sensing circuit: the output p.d. changes with temperature or light.\n\n- **LDR in a dark-detecting circuit:** in the dark the LDR's resistance rises, so it takes a larger share of the p.d.; the output across the LDR rises and can switch on a street light.\n- **Thermistor in a fire alarm:** when hot, the thermistor's resistance falls, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor rises and can trigger an alarm.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃ + … | R: combined resistance, Ω | Resistors in series. | Core |\n| total e.m.f. = E₁ + E₂ + … | V | Cells in series, all facing the same way. | Core |\n| I is the same everywhere in a series circuit | A | Find the current once, use it for every component. | Core |\n| V = IR | V: V; I: A; R: Ω | The p.d. across any single component, or the whole circuit. | Core |\n| I = I₁ + I₂ | A | Current from the source equals the sum of branch currents (junction rule). | Supplement |\n| V = V₁ + V₂ | V | P.d.s in series add up to the supply p.d. | Supplement |\n| p.d. across each parallel branch = p.d. across the arrangement | V | Parallel branches. | Supplement |\n| 1 ÷ R = 1 ÷ R₁ + 1 ÷ R₂ | R₁, R₂: resistors in parallel, Ω | Two resistors in parallel. For two only: R = (R₁ × R₂) ÷ (R₁ + R₂). | Supplement |\n| R₁ ÷ R₂ = V₁ ÷ V₂ | V₁, V₂: p.d.s across R₁ and R₂ in series, V | Potential dividers. With supply V: V₁ = V × R₁ ÷ (R₁ + R₂). | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: series circuit\n\n**Example — find everything.** A 12 V battery is connected to a 4.0 Ω resistor and an 8.0 Ω resistor in series.\n\n- R = 4.0 + 8.0 = 12 Ω.\n- I = V ÷ R = 12 ÷ 12 = 1.0 A, the same through both.\n- V across 4.0 Ω = 1.0 × 4.0 = 4.0 V. V across 8.0 Ω = 1.0 × 8.0 = 8.0 V.\n- Check: 4.0 + 8.0 = 12 V, the supply p.d.\n\n**Example — cells in series.** Three 1.5 V cells in series give 4.5 V. If one is reversed: 1.5 + 1.5 − 1.5 = 1.5 V.\n\n### Situation: two resistors in parallel (Supplement)\n\n**Example — combined resistance.** 6.0 Ω and 3.0 Ω in parallel.\n\n- 1 ÷ R = 1 ÷ 6.0 + 1 ÷ 3.0 = 0.167 + 0.333 = 0.500, so R = 2.0 Ω.\n- Or: R = (6.0 × 3.0) ÷ (6.0 + 3.0) = 18 ÷ 9.0 = 2.0 Ω. Less than either resistor, as expected.\n\n**Example — branch currents.** The pair above is connected to a 12 V supply.\n\n- Each branch has 12 V across it.\n- I through 6.0 Ω = 12 ÷ 6.0 = 2.0 A; I through 3.0 Ω = 12 ÷ 3.0 = 4.0 A.\n- Current from the supply = 2.0 + 4.0 = 6.0 A. Check: 12 ÷ 2.0 Ω = 6.0 A.\n\n**Example — two equal resistors.** Two 10 Ω resistors in parallel: R = 100 ÷ 20 = 5.0 Ω, half of one.\n\n### Situation: junction rule (Supplement)\n\n**Example — missing current.** 2.5 A enters a junction; one branch carries 0.9 A. The other carries 2.5 − 0.9 = 1.6 A.\n\n### Situation: series and parallel combined (Supplement)\n\n**Example.** A 4.0 Ω resistor is in series with a parallel pair of 12 Ω and 6.0 Ω, connected to a 9.0 V supply.\n\n- Parallel pair: (12 × 6.0) ÷ (12 + 6.0) = 72 ÷ 18 = 4.0 Ω.\n- Total resistance = 4.0 + 4.0 = 8.0 Ω.\n- Supply current = 9.0 ÷ 8.0 = 1.125 A ≈ 1.1 A.\n- P.d. across the 4.0 Ω series resistor = 1.125 × 4.0 = 4.5 V; across the parallel pair = 9.0 − 4.5 = 4.5 V.\n- Current in the 12 Ω branch = 4.5 ÷ 12 = 0.375 A; in the 6.0 Ω branch = 4.5 ÷ 6.0 = 0.75 A. Check: 0.375 + 0.75 = 1.125 A.\n\n### Situation: potential divider (Supplement)\n\n**Example — output p.d.** R₁ = 2.0 kΩ and R₂ = 8.0 kΩ in series across 10 V.\n\n- V₁ ÷ V₂ = R₁ ÷ R₂ = 2.0 ÷ 8.0 = 1 : 4, so the 10 V is split 2.0 V and 8.0 V.\n- Or: V₂ = 10 × 8.0 ÷ (2.0 + 8.0) = 8.0 V.\n\n**Example — find a resistor.** A 6.0 V supply across a 1.0 kΩ resistor and an LDR in series. In the dark the p.d. across the LDR is 5.0 V.\n\n- P.d. across the resistor = 6.0 − 5.0 = 1.0 V.\n- R(LDR) ÷ 1.0 kΩ = 5.0 ÷ 1.0, so R(LDR) = 5.0 kΩ.\n\n**Example — sensor behaviour.** A thermistor is in series with a fixed resistor; the output is taken across the fixed resistor. As temperature rises, the thermistor's resistance falls, its share of the p.d. falls, so the p.d. across the fixed resistor (the output) rises.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Series: same current, p.d.s add. Parallel: same p.d., currents add.\n- Two resistors in parallel always combine to less than the smaller one; use product ÷ sum.\n- Find the total resistance and supply current first, then work out each part.\n- A thermistor's resistance falls as it gets hotter; an LDR's falls as it gets brighter.\n- Check your answers: branch currents should add to the supply current; series p.d.s should add to the supply p.d.\n- Explain lamps in parallel: full p.d. each, independent switching, others stay on if one fails.\n",
  "igcse-u17": "# Unit 17 — Electrical safety\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, section 4.4, Core. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Hazards — 4.4\n\n- **Damaged insulation:** exposed live wires can give an electric shock if touched, or cause a short circuit and fire.\n- **Overheating cables:** a cable carrying more current than it is designed for gets hot; the insulation can melt and start a fire. Thin cables overheat at lower currents than thick ones.\n- **Damp conditions:** water (especially impure water) conducts, so it can provide a path for current through a person; wet skin has a lower resistance, so a shock is more dangerous.\n- **Overloading:** plugging too many appliances into one socket, extension lead or adaptor draws a large total current, which overheats the cables and plug, risking fire.\n\n### Mains wiring — 4.4\n\nA mains circuit has three wires:\n\n- **Live (line) wire:** carries the alternating p.d. (about 230 V in many countries). It is the dangerous wire.\n- **Neutral wire:** completes the circuit; it is kept at or near 0 V.\n- **Earth wire:** a safety wire connected to the ground; it normally carries no current.\n\nSwitches (and fuses) must be connected in the live wire. Then, when the switch is off, the appliance is disconnected from the high p.d. If the switch were in the neutral wire, the appliance would still be connected to the live wire even when switched off, and touching a fault inside it could give a shock.\n\n### Fuses and trip switches — 4.4\n\nA **fuse** is a thin wire in a cartridge. If the current rises above its rating, the wire heats up and melts, breaking the circuit. It protects the cables and appliance from overheating and fire. Once blown, it must be replaced.\n\nA **trip switch (circuit breaker)** uses an electromagnet: a current above its setting makes the electromagnet strong enough to pull a switch open. It acts faster than a fuse and can be reset by pressing a button once the fault is fixed.\n\n**Choosing a rating:** pick the smallest standard value that is just above the normal working current. Common fuse ratings are 3 A, 5 A and 13 A. A fuse rated too low blows during normal use; one rated far too high does not protect against a fault.\n\n### Earthing and double insulation — 4.4\n\nAn appliance with a metal casing must be **earthed**: the earth wire connects the casing to the ground. If a fault lets the live wire touch the casing, a large current flows through the low-resistance earth wire. This blows the fuse (or trips the breaker), disconnecting the live supply, so the casing is never left at a dangerous p.d. that could give a shock.\n\nA **double-insulated** appliance has a casing made of plastic (an insulator), and its live parts are insulated a second time inside. The casing cannot become live, so no earth wire is needed. A double-insulated appliance still has a fuse in the live wire: the fuse (without an earth wire) protects the circuit and the cable from overheating if too much current flows. The symbol is a square inside a square.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I = P ÷ V | I: normal current, A; P: power rating, W; V: mains p.d., V | Find the working current of an appliance, then choose a fuse. From P = IV (Unit 15). | Core |\n| fuse rating = next standard value above the working current | A | Choosing a fuse or trip-switch setting. | Core |\n| total current = sum of the currents of all appliances on the socket | A | Deciding whether a socket or extension lead is overloaded. | Core |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: choose a fuse\n\n**Example — kettle.** A 2.8 kW kettle on 230 V. I = 2800 ÷ 230 = 12.2 A. Choose a 13 A fuse (3 A and 5 A would blow at once).\n\n**Example — lamp.** A 60 W lamp on 230 V. I = 60 ÷ 230 = 0.26 A. Choose a 3 A fuse.\n\n**Example — television.** A 920 W appliance on 230 V. I = 920 ÷ 230 = 4.0 A. Choose a 5 A fuse.\n\n### Situation: overloaded extension lead\n\n**Example.** An extension lead rated 13 A has a 2.0 kW heater, a 1.2 kW iron and a 0.3 kW computer plugged in, on 230 V.\n\n- Total power = 3.5 kW = 3500 W.\n- Total current = 3500 ÷ 230 = 15.2 A, above 13 A, so the lead is overloaded and its fuse should blow (or it may overheat).\n\n### Situation: trip switch setting\n\n**Example.** A lighting circuit has ten 100 W lamps on 230 V. Total current = 1000 ÷ 230 = 4.3 A. A 6 A trip switch would be suitable; a 32 A one would not protect the thin lighting cables.\n\n### Situation: explaining a fault\n\n**Example — live wire touches a metal casing.** With an earth wire: a large current flows from live to earth, the fuse melts, the supply is cut off, and the casing is safe. Without an earth wire: the casing stays at the live p.d.; a person touching it provides a path to earth and gets a shock.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Switches and fuses go in the live wire. Explain why: off means disconnected from the high p.d.\n- Earthing works together with the fuse: the earth gives a low-resistance path, the large current blows the fuse.\n- A fuse protects the cables and appliance from overheating; it does not prevent a shock on its own.\n- Choose the fuse just above the working current, not equal to it and not far above it.\n- Double-insulated: plastic casing, no earth wire needed, but a fuse is still used.\n",
  "igcse-u18": "# Unit 18 — Electromagnetic effects\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 4.5.1, 4.5.2, 4.5.3, 4.5.4, 4.5.5 and 4.5.6, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Electromagnetic induction — 4.5.1\n\nAn e.m.f. is induced in a conductor when it moves across a magnetic field (cutting field lines), or when the magnetic field linking it changes. If the conductor is part of a complete circuit, the induced e.m.f. drives a current.\n\n**Experiment:** connect a coil to a sensitive centre-zero meter (galvanometer). Push a bar magnet into the coil: the needle flicks one way. Hold it still inside: no reading. Pull it out: the needle flicks the other way. Moving the coil instead of the magnet has the same effect. A straight wire moved up and down between the poles of a magnet also gives a reading.\n\nThe size of the induced e.m.f. increases with:\n\n- the speed of the movement (the rate at which field lines are cut or the field changes);\n- the strength of the magnetic field;\n- the number of turns on the coil.\n\n**Supplement:** the direction of the induced e.m.f. opposes the change causing it. Push the N pole of a magnet into a coil and the induced current makes the near end of the coil an N pole, which repels the magnet. Pull it out and that end becomes an S pole, attracting the magnet back. You must do work against this opposing force, and that work is the energy that becomes electrical energy.\n\n**Supplement — relative directions (Fleming's right-hand rule):** for a wire moved through a magnetic field, hold the thumb, first finger and second finger of the right hand at right angles. Thumb = motion (direction of the force moving the wire), First finger = Field (N to S), seCond finger = induced Current (conventional).\n\n### The a.c. generator — 4.5.2 (Supplement)\n\nA coil of wire rotates in a magnetic field (or a magnet rotates inside a coil). As the coil turns, its sides cut the field lines, inducing an alternating e.m.f. Each end of the coil is connected to its own slip ring, which turns with the coil; carbon brushes press against the slip rings to take the current to the external circuit without the wires twisting.\n\n**Graph of e.m.f. against time:** a smooth wave (sinusoidal), positive then negative each turn.\n\n- **Peak e.m.f. (positive or negative):** when the plane of the coil is parallel to the magnetic field lines. Its sides are moving at right angles to the field, cutting the lines fastest.\n- **Zero e.m.f.:** when the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field lines. Its sides are moving along the field lines, not cutting them.\n- One complete rotation gives one complete cycle: a peak, zero, a trough, zero.\n\nTurning faster gives a larger peak e.m.f. and more cycles per second (higher frequency). A stronger magnet or more turns gives a larger peak at the same frequency.\n\n### Magnetic effect of a current — 4.5.3\n\nA current in a wire produces a magnetic field around it.\n\n- **Straight wire:** the field lines are concentric circles around the wire, in planes at right angles to it. Direction by the right-hand grip rule: grip the wire with the thumb pointing along the conventional current; the fingers curl in the direction of the field.\n- **Solenoid (coil):** the field outside is like that of a bar magnet; inside, the lines are straight, parallel and close together (a strong, uniform field). Grip the coil with the fingers curling in the direction of the current; the thumb points to the N pole.\n\n**Experiment:** pass a vertical wire through a horizontal card. Sprinkle iron filings on the card and tap it while a large current flows: the filings form circles. Place plotting compasses on the card to show the direction; reverse the current and the compasses reverse. Do the same with a solenoid threaded through a card.\n\n**Supplement:** the field around a straight wire is strongest close to the wire and gets weaker further away (the circles are drawn further apart). The field of a solenoid is strongest inside. Increasing the current makes either field stronger; reversing the current reverses the field direction. For a solenoid, more turns (and a soft-iron core) also make the field stronger.\n\n**Uses:**\n\n- **Relay:** a small current through a coil magnetises a soft-iron core, which attracts an iron armature and closes a pair of contacts in a second circuit. A small, safe current can then switch a large current, for example a car starter motor or a high-power heater controlled by a thermostat.\n- **Loudspeaker:** an alternating current from an amplifier flows in a coil attached to a paper cone and placed in the field of a permanent magnet. The force on the coil reverses as the current reverses, so the cone vibrates at the frequency of the current, producing sound.\n- **Electric bell, electromagnetic lock, scrap-yard crane.**\n\n### Force on a current-carrying conductor — 4.5.4\n\nA wire carrying a current in a magnetic field experiences a force (the motor effect). The force is largest when the wire is at right angles to the field, and zero when the wire is parallel to the field.\n\n**Experiment:** place a loose wire (or a rolling rod on two rails) between the poles of a horseshoe magnet and pass a current: the wire jumps (or rolls). Reverse the current, and the force reverses. Reverse the field (turn the magnet round), and the force reverses. Reverse both, and the force is in the original direction.\n\n**Supplement — relative directions (Fleming's left-hand rule):** hold the thumb, first finger and second finger of the left hand at right angles. First finger = Field (N to S), seCond finger = conventional Current, thuMb = Motion (force).\n\n**Supplement — beams of charged particles:** a beam of moving charged particles is a current, so it is deflected by a magnetic field in the same way. For a beam of positive particles, the conventional current is in the direction they travel. For electrons, the conventional current is opposite to their direction of travel, so point the second finger backwards along the beam.\n\n### The d.c. motor — 4.5.5\n\nA current-carrying coil in a magnetic field experiences a turning effect: the forces on its two sides are in opposite directions (one up, one down), producing a moment. The turning effect increases with:\n\n- more turns on the coil;\n- a larger current;\n- a stronger magnetic field.\n\n**Supplement — operation:** a split-ring commutator connects the coil to the d.c. supply through two brushes. Every half-turn, just as the coil passes the vertical position, the split ring swaps the coil's connections, reversing the current in the coil. The forces then keep turning the coil in the same direction. Without the commutator the coil would rock back and forth and stop. The coil's momentum carries it past the vertical, where the turning effect is momentarily zero.\n\n### The transformer — 4.5.6\n\nA transformer is two coils of insulated wire, the primary and the secondary, wound on the same soft-iron core.\n\n- **Step-up transformer:** more turns on the secondary than the primary; the output p.d. is greater than the input.\n- **Step-down transformer:** fewer turns on the secondary; the output p.d. is smaller.\n\n**High-voltage transmission:** at the power station, a step-up transformer raises the p.d. to hundreds of thousands of volts for the transmission cables; near homes, step-down transformers reduce it to the mains p.d. Advantages of high-voltage transmission: for the same power, the current is much smaller, so less energy is wasted heating the cables, and thinner, cheaper cables (with fewer pylons) can be used.\n\n**Supplement — principle:** an alternating current in the primary coil produces an alternating magnetic field in the soft-iron core. This changing field passes through (links) the secondary coil and induces an alternating e.m.f. in it. A transformer only works with a.c.: a steady d.c. produces a steady field, which induces nothing. Soft iron is used because it is easily magnetised and demagnetised as the field reverses.\n\n**Supplement:** in an ideal (100% efficient) transformer the power in equals the power out, so stepping up the p.d. steps down the current by the same factor. Power lost in cables is P = I²R: halving the current reduces the loss to a quarter.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| V_p ÷ V_s = N_p ÷ N_s | V_p, V_s: primary and secondary p.d., V; N_p, N_s: number of turns on each coil | Any transformer. Rearranged: V_s = V_p × N_s ÷ N_p. | Core |\n| I_p × V_p = I_s × V_s | I_p, I_s: primary and secondary current, A | 100% efficient transformer: power in = power out. | Supplement |\n| P = I²R | P: power lost as heating, W; I: current in cable, A; R: cable resistance, Ω | Energy wasted in transmission cables. | Supplement |\n| P = IV | W | Power transmitted (from Unit 15); gives the current for a given power and p.d. | Core |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: find the output p.d.\n\n**Example — step-down.** A transformer has 2000 turns on the primary and 100 on the secondary; the input is 230 V a.c. V_s = 230 × 100 ÷ 2000 = 11.5 V.\n\n**Example — step-up.** 400 primary turns, 20 000 secondary turns, input 25 000 V. V_s = 25 000 × 20 000 ÷ 400 = 1 250 000 V. (Real grid transformers step up to about 400 000 V.)\n\n### Situation: find the number of turns\n\n**Example — phone charger.** A charger steps 230 V down to 5.75 V using a primary of 4000 turns. N_s = N_p × V_s ÷ V_p = 4000 × 5.75 ÷ 230 = 100 turns.\n\n### Situation: currents in an ideal transformer (Supplement)\n\n**Example — find the primary current.** A 100% efficient transformer gives 12 V and 5.0 A to a lamp from a 240 V supply.\n\n- Power out = 12 × 5.0 = 60 W = power in.\n- I_p = 60 ÷ 240 = 0.25 A.\n\n### Situation: power loss in cables (Supplement)\n\n**Example — compare two p.d.s.** A power station sends 1.0 MW through cables of total resistance 5.0 Ω.\n\n- At 10 000 V: I = P ÷ V = 1 000 000 ÷ 10 000 = 100 A. Loss = I²R = 100² × 5.0 = 50 000 W (5% of the power).\n- At 100 000 V: I = 10 A. Loss = 10² × 5.0 = 500 W (0.05%).\n- Ten times the p.d. gives one tenth of the current and one hundredth of the power loss.\n\n### Situation: predict a direction (Supplement)\n\n**Example — motor effect.** A wire runs from left to right through a field pointing into the page, with conventional current left to right. Left hand: first finger into the page, second finger to the right; the thumb points up. The force on the wire is upwards.\n\n**Example — electron beam.** Electrons move left to right through the same field. The conventional current is right to left, so the force is downwards.\n\n**Example — generator.** A wire is pushed upward through a field pointing into the page. Right hand: thumb up, first finger into the page; the second finger points left, so the induced current flows from right to left.\n\n### Situation: reading a generator graph (Supplement)\n\n**Example.** The e.m.f. peaks at 4 V every 0.10 s (one peak per cycle). The frequency is 1 ÷ 0.10 = 10 Hz, so the coil turns 10 times a second. Doubling the rotation speed doubles the frequency to 20 Hz and roughly doubles the peak to 8 V.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Induction needs relative movement or a changing field. A magnet held still in a coil induces nothing.\n- Factors for a larger induced e.m.f.: faster motion, stronger field, more turns.\n- Left hand for motors (force), right hand for generators (induced current). Use conventional current.\n- Slip rings: a.c. generator. Split-ring commutator: d.c. motor.\n- Transformers need a.c. and a soft-iron core. Step-up raises p.d. and lowers current.\n- Explain high-voltage transmission: lower current, so less I²R heating in the cables.\n",
  "igcse-u19": "# Unit 19 — The nuclear model of the atom\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 5.1.1 and 5.1.2, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### The atom — 5.1.1\n\nAn atom has a tiny, positively charged nucleus at its centre, with negatively charged electrons in orbit around it. Most of an atom is empty space. A neutral atom has equal numbers of protons and electrons, so the charges balance.\n\n**Ions:** an atom that loses one or more electrons becomes a positive ion; an atom that gains electrons becomes a negative ion. The nucleus does not change.\n\n### Evidence for the nucleus: alpha scattering — 5.1.1 (Supplement)\n\nAlpha particles (small, positive, fast) were fired at a very thin sheet of gold foil in a vacuum, and a detector recorded where they went.\n\n| Observation | Conclusion |\n|---|---|\n| Almost all alpha particles went straight through, undeflected | The atom is mostly empty space; the nucleus is very small |\n| A few were deflected through small angles | The nucleus is positively charged: it repels the positive alpha particles that pass close to it |\n| A very few (about 1 in 8000) bounced back through more than 90° | The nucleus contains most of the mass of the atom, concentrated in a tiny volume, so a head-on alpha particle is turned back |\n\nThis replaced the earlier \"plum pudding\" model, in which positive charge was spread through the whole atom.\n\n### The nucleus — 5.1.2\n\nThe nucleus is made of protons and neutrons, together called nucleons.\n\n| Particle | Relative charge | Relative mass | Where |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Proton | +1 | 1 | Nucleus |\n| Neutron | 0 | 1 | Nucleus |\n| Electron | −1 | about 1/1840 (negligible) | Orbiting the nucleus |\n\n- **Proton number (atomic number), Z:** the number of protons in the nucleus. It decides which element the atom is.\n- **Nucleon number (mass number), A:** the total number of protons and neutrons.\n- **Number of neutrons, N = A − Z.**\n\n**Nuclide notation:** the nucleon number is written at the top left of the element's symbol and the proton number at the bottom left. For example `^12_6 C` is carbon with 6 protons and 12 − 6 = 6 neutrons; `^235_92 U` is uranium with 92 protons and 143 neutrons.\n\n**Isotopes** are atoms of the same element (same proton number) with different numbers of neutrons (different nucleon numbers). An element may have several isotopes: `^12_6 C`, `^13_6 C` and `^14_6 C` are all carbon. Isotopes have the same chemical properties, because they have the same number of electrons, but different masses, and some are radioactive.\n\n**Supplement:** the relative charge of a nucleus equals its proton number Z (each proton is +1, neutrons are 0). The relative mass of a nucleus equals its nucleon number A (each nucleon has a relative mass of 1).\n\n### Fission and fusion — 5.1.2 (Supplement)\n\n**Nuclear fission** is the splitting of a large, unstable nucleus into two smaller nuclei (daughter nuclei), usually after it absorbs a neutron. Two or three neutrons are also released, along with a large amount of energy. The released neutrons can cause further fissions: a chain reaction, controlled in a nuclear power station.\n\n`^1_0 n + ^235_92 U → ^141_56 Ba + ^92_36 Kr + 3 ^1_0 n`\n\n**Nuclear fusion** is the joining of two light nuclei to form a heavier nucleus, releasing a large amount of energy. It happens in stars, where hydrogen nuclei fuse to make helium; it needs a very high temperature and pressure so the positive nuclei move fast enough to overcome their repulsion.\n\n`^2_1 H + ^3_1 H → ^4_2 He + ^1_0 n`\n\nIn both processes the total mass of the products is slightly less than the total mass before. This \"lost\" mass has been converted into energy, released mainly as kinetic energy of the products. In every nuclear equation, the nucleon numbers balance and the proton numbers balance.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| N = A − Z | N: neutron number; A: nucleon number; Z: proton number (no units) | Counting the particles in a nucleus. | Core |\n| electrons in a neutral atom = Z | | Counting electrons. For an ion: subtract the positive charge or add the negative charge. | Core |\n| relative charge of nucleus = +Z | | Comparing nuclei. | Supplement |\n| relative mass of nucleus = A | | Comparing nuclei. | Supplement |\n| sum of A before = sum of A after; sum of Z before = sum of Z after | | Balancing fission and fusion equations; finding a missing nucleus. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: count the particles\n\n**Example — sodium.** `^23_11 Na` has 11 protons, 23 − 11 = 12 neutrons, and in a neutral atom 11 electrons.\n\n**Example — an ion.** A `^27_13 Al` ion with a charge of +3 has 13 protons, 14 neutrons and 13 − 3 = 10 electrons.\n\n**Example — a negative ion.** A `^35_17 Cl` ion with charge −1 has 17 protons, 18 neutrons and 17 + 1 = 18 electrons.\n\n### Situation: identify isotopes\n\n**Example.** Which two of these are isotopes of the same element: `^14_6 X`, `^14_7 Y`, `^12_6 Z`? X and Z: both have proton number 6. Y has the same nucleon number as X but a different proton number, so it is a different element.\n\n### Situation: write a nuclide from a description\n\n**Example.** A nucleus has 26 protons and 30 neutrons and is iron, Fe. A = 26 + 30 = 56, so it is `^56_26 Fe`.\n\n### Situation: complete a fission equation (Supplement)\n\n**Example — find the missing nucleus.** `^1_0 n + ^235_92 U → ^144_55 Cs + ? + 2 ^1_0 n`\n\n- Nucleon numbers: 1 + 235 = 236 = 144 + A + 2, so A = 90.\n- Proton numbers: 0 + 92 = 92 = 55 + Z + 0, so Z = 37 (rubidium).\n- Missing nucleus: `^90_37 Rb`.\n\n### Situation: complete a fusion equation (Supplement)\n\n**Example.** `^2_1 H + ^2_1 H → ? + ^1_0 n`\n\n- A: 2 + 2 = 4 = A + 1, so A = 3. Z: 1 + 1 = 2 = Z + 0, so Z = 2.\n- Missing nucleus: `^3_2 He` (helium-3).\n\n### Situation: compare charge and mass (Supplement)\n\n**Example.** Compare `^238_92 U` and `^4_2 He`. Relative charges: +92 and +2. Relative masses: 238 and 4. The uranium nucleus has 46 times the charge and about 60 times the mass.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Nucleon number on top, proton number below.\n- Neutrons = nucleon number − proton number.\n- Isotopes: same protons, different neutrons. Same element, same chemistry.\n- Ions change the number of electrons, never the nucleus.\n- Alpha scattering: \"most went straight through\" means mostly empty space; \"a few bounced back\" means a small, dense, positive nucleus.\n- In fission and fusion equations, balance both the top numbers and the bottom numbers; mass decreases slightly and energy is released.\n",
  "igcse-u20": "# Unit 20 — Radioactivity\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 5.2.1, 5.2.2, 5.2.3, 5.2.4 and 5.2.5, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### Background radiation and detection — 5.2.1\n\nBackground radiation is the low level of ionising radiation that is around us all the time, from natural and artificial sources. Significant sources:\n\n- radon gas in the air (from rocks in the ground; often the largest contributor);\n- rocks and buildings (granite, bricks);\n- food and drink (all living things contain some radioactive isotopes, such as carbon-14 and potassium-40);\n- cosmic rays from space.\n\nIonising radiation is measured with a detector (such as a Geiger–Müller tube) connected to a counter or ratemeter. The count rate is the number of counts per second (counts/s) or per minute (counts/minute).\n\n**Supplement:** a measured count rate includes the background. Measure the background count rate with the source removed (ideally over a long time), then subtract it: corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate.\n\n### The three types of nuclear emission — 5.2.2\n\nEmission from a nucleus is spontaneous (it happens by itself; nothing triggers it) and random in direction.\n\n| | Alpha (α) | Beta (β) | Gamma (γ) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Nature | Helium nucleus: 2 protons + 2 neutrons | Fast-moving electron, emitted from the nucleus | Electromagnetic radiation (a very short wavelength wave) |\n| Charge | +2 | −1 | 0 |\n| Relative mass | 4 | about 1/1840 | 0 |\n| Ionising effect | Strong | Moderate | Weak |\n| Penetration (stopped by) | Weak: a sheet of paper, a few cm of air, skin | Moderate: a few mm of aluminium | Strong: reduced (never fully stopped) by thick lead or several metres of concrete |\n\n(β here always means β⁻; β⁺ is not included.)\n\n**Supplement — deflection in electric and magnetic fields:**\n\n- Alpha and beta particles are charged, so they are deflected; they bend in opposite directions because their charges are opposite.\n- Beta particles are deflected much more than alpha particles, because their mass is so much smaller (even though their charge is half the size).\n- Gamma rays are uncharged, so they are not deflected.\n- In an electric field between charged plates: alpha bends towards the negative plate, beta towards the positive plate. In a magnetic field: use Fleming's left-hand rule, treating beta as a current in the opposite direction to its motion.\n\n**Supplement — why ionising effects differ:** ionisation happens when the radiation knocks electrons off atoms. Alpha particles have a large charge (+2) and, being heavy, travel relatively slowly with a lot of kinetic energy, so they interact strongly with every atom they pass and cause a great deal of ionisation. They lose their kinetic energy quickly, which is why they penetrate so little. Beta particles have less charge (−1) and move faster, so they ionise less and travel further. Gamma rays have no charge, so they ionise least and penetrate most.\n\n### Radioactive decay — 5.2.3\n\nRadioactive decay is a change in an unstable nucleus that results in the emission of alpha or beta particles and/or gamma radiation. It is spontaneous and random: you cannot predict which nucleus will decay next, or when, and nothing (temperature, pressure, chemical reaction) changes the rate.\n\nDuring alpha or beta decay the number of protons changes, so the nucleus becomes a different element.\n\n**Supplement — why nuclei are unstable:** an isotope may be radioactive because it has too many neutrons for its number of protons, or because the nucleus is too heavy (too many nucleons). Decay makes the nucleus more stable.\n\n- **Alpha decay:** the nucleus loses 2 protons and 2 neutrons. Nucleon number falls by 4; proton number falls by 2. Typical of heavy nuclei.\n- **Beta decay:** a neutron in the nucleus changes into a proton and an electron; the electron is emitted at high speed. Nucleon number stays the same; proton number rises by 1. This reduces the number of excess neutrons.\n- **Gamma emission:** the nucleus loses energy as a gamma ray, often just after alpha or beta decay. The nucleon and proton numbers do not change.\n\n**Supplement — decay equations:**\n\n- Alpha: `^A_Z X → ^(A−4)_(Z−2) Y + ^4_2 α`\n- Beta: `^A_Z X → ^A_(Z+1) Y + ^0_-1 β`\n- Gamma: `^A_Z X → ^A_Z X + γ`\n- In beta decay, the change inside the nucleus is `^1_0 n → ^1_1 p + ^0_-1 e`.\n\n### Half-life — 5.2.4\n\nThe half-life of an isotope is the time taken for half the nuclei of that isotope in any sample to decay. After each half-life, the number of undecayed nuclei, the activity, and the count rate (after background is subtracted) all halve. Each isotope has its own half-life, from fractions of a second to billions of years.\n\nAfter 1 half-life: ½ remains. After 2: ¼. After 3: ⅛. After 4: 1/16. After n half-lives: (½)ⁿ of the original remains.\n\nOn a decay curve (count rate against time), read the time for the count rate to fall from any value to half that value; repeat from different starting points and take the mean.\n\n**Supplement:** if the data include background radiation, subtract the background from every reading first, then find the half-life. A curve that levels off at a constant non-zero value shows the background count rate.\n\n**Supplement — choosing an isotope for a job:** the type of radiation decides what it can pass through and how much harm it does; the half-life decides how long it stays active.\n\n| Application | Radiation needed | Half-life needed | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Household smoke alarm | Alpha | Long (years; americium-241 has about 430 years) | Alpha ionises the air in a small gap, letting a tiny current flow; smoke absorbs the alpha, the current drops and the alarm sounds. Alpha cannot escape the alarm's casing. A long half-life means no replacement is needed. |\n| Irradiating food to kill bacteria | Gamma | Long enough to last (cobalt-60, about 5 years) | Gamma penetrates the packaging and the food and kills microorganisms; the food does not become radioactive. |\n| Sterilising medical equipment | Gamma | Fairly long | Penetrates sealed packaging, so equipment stays sterile until opened. |\n| Measuring and controlling thickness | Beta for paper, foil or thin plastic; gamma for thick metal sheets | Long, so the count rate does not fall during use | The source is on one side, a detector on the other. If the sheet is too thick, fewer particles get through, and the rollers adjust. Alpha would be stopped completely; gamma would pass through paper almost unaffected. |\n| Diagnosing cancer (tracer) | Gamma | Short (hours; technetium-99m about 6 hours) | Gamma passes out of the body to a detector; a short half-life means the patient is not exposed for long. |\n| Treating cancer | Gamma | Long, for the external source (cobalt-60) | Beams from several directions meet at the tumour to kill its cells, limiting the dose to healthy tissue. |\n\n### Safety — 5.2.5\n\nIonising radiation can damage living cells: it can kill cells (cell death), change their DNA (mutations), and cause cancer. Alpha is most dangerous inside the body (swallowed or breathed in), because it is strongly ionising; outside the body, beta and gamma are more dangerous, because they can reach the organs.\n\nRadioactive materials are moved, used and stored safely by:\n\n- keeping them in lead-lined containers, labelled with the hazard symbol, locked away when not in use;\n- handling them with long tongs, never with bare hands, and pointing them away from people;\n- using them for the shortest possible time;\n- wearing a film badge or dosimeter to monitor exposure (workers), and protective clothing or gloves.\n\n**Supplement:** all these precautions work by:\n\n- **reducing exposure time:** less time near the source means less radiation absorbed;\n- **increasing distance:** radiation spreads out (and alpha and beta are absorbed by air), so the dose falls rapidly with distance;\n- **using shielding:** materials such as lead, concrete or thick glass absorb the radiation before it reaches people.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| fraction remaining = (½)ⁿ | n: number of half-lives = time ÷ half-life | How much is left after a whole number of half-lives. | Core |\n| number of half-lives = total time ÷ half-life | times in the same unit | Converting a time into half-lives. | Core |\n| corrected count rate = measured count rate − background count rate | counts/s or counts/minute | Every reading that includes background. | Supplement |\n| alpha: A − 4, Z − 2; beta: A unchanged, Z + 1; gamma: no change | A: nucleon number; Z: proton number | Writing and completing decay equations. | Supplement |\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: how much is left\n\n**Example — mass remaining.** A sample contains 80 g of an isotope with a half-life of 5 days. How much remains after 20 days?\n\n- Number of half-lives = 20 ÷ 5 = 4.\n- 80 → 40 → 20 → 10 → 5 g. (Or 80 × (½)⁴ = 5 g.)\n\n**Example — count rate.** A source has a count rate of 640 counts/minute and a half-life of 2 hours. After 6 hours: 640 → 320 → 160 → 80 counts/minute.\n\n### Situation: find the half-life from data\n\n**Example — table.** A count rate falls from 1200 counts/s to 150 counts/s in 36 minutes.\n\n- 1200 → 600 → 300 → 150 is 3 half-lives.\n- Half-life = 36 ÷ 3 = 12 minutes.\n\n**Example — fraction.** After 24 years, 1/8 of a sample remains. 1/8 = (½)³, so 3 half-lives = 24 years and the half-life is 8 years.\n\n### Situation: find the time taken\n\n**Example.** An isotope has a half-life of 3.0 hours. How long until its activity falls to 1/16 of the starting value? 1/16 = (½)⁴, so 4 × 3.0 = 12 hours.\n\n### Situation: background radiation (Supplement)\n\n**Example — corrected count rate.** A detector reads 85 counts/minute near a source; the background is 25 counts/minute. Corrected count rate = 85 − 25 = 60 counts/minute.\n\n**Example — half-life with background.** The background is 20 counts/minute. A source reads 420 counts/minute at the start and 120 counts/minute after 10 minutes.\n\n- Corrected: 420 − 20 = 400 at the start; 120 − 20 = 100 after 10 minutes.\n- 400 → 200 → 100 is 2 half-lives, so the half-life = 10 ÷ 2 = 5 minutes.\n- (Without correcting, 420 → 120 would not be a whole number of half-lives, and the answer would be wrong.)\n\n### Situation: identify the radiation from absorption data\n\n**Example.** Count rates (background subtracted): no absorber 500; paper 495; 3 mm aluminium 300; 3 mm lead 150. Paper barely changes the count, so there is no alpha. Aluminium removes some, so there is beta. Some still passes through aluminium and lead, so there is gamma too.\n\n### Situation: decay equations (Supplement)\n\n**Example — alpha decay.** Radium-226 (Z = 88) emits an alpha particle: `^226_88 Ra → ^222_86 Rn + ^4_2 α`. Nucleon number 226 = 222 + 4; proton number 88 = 86 + 2.\n\n**Example — beta decay.** Carbon-14 (Z = 6) emits a beta particle: `^14_6 C → ^14_7 N + ^0_-1 β`. Nucleon number unchanged; proton number 6 = 7 + (−1).\n\n**Example — a decay chain.** Uranium-238 (Z = 92) emits an alpha, then a beta, then another beta. After alpha: A = 234, Z = 90. After the first beta: A = 234, Z = 91. After the second beta: A = 234, Z = 92: an isotope of uranium again, `^234_92 U`.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- Always subtract background before using count rates (Supplement), and say so.\n- Half-life questions: count the halvings. Check the time units.\n- Alpha: stopped by paper, most ionising. Beta: stopped by aluminium. Gamma: reduced by thick lead, least ionising.\n- In fields, alpha and beta bend opposite ways, beta more; gamma goes straight.\n- Alpha decay: A − 4, Z − 2. Beta decay: Z + 1, A the same. Gamma: no change.\n- Choosing a source: match the penetration to the job, and the half-life to how long it is needed (short inside the body, long for equipment).\n- Safety: time, distance, shielding.\n",
  "igcse-u21": "# Unit 21 — Space physics\n\nSource: Cambridge IGCSE Physics 0625 syllabus, sections 6.1.1, 6.1.2, 6.2.1, 6.2.2 and 6.2.3, Core and Supplement. See [source and scope](README.md).\n\n## Concept summary\n\n### The Earth, Sun and Moon — 6.1.1\n\n- **Day and night:** the Earth spins on its axis once in about 24 hours. The half facing the Sun has day; the other half has night. The Sun appears to rise in the east, cross the sky and set in the west because the Earth rotates.\n- **Seasons:** the Earth orbits the Sun once in about 365 days, and its axis is tilted (by about 23.5°). When a hemisphere is tilted towards the Sun, it has summer: the Sun is higher in the sky, its rays are more concentrated, and days are longer. Six months later, that hemisphere is tilted away and has winter.\n- **Phases of the Moon:** the Moon orbits the Earth in about one month. We only see the part of its sunlit half that faces us, so as it moves round its orbit it appears to change shape: new moon, crescent, quarter, gibbous, full, and back again. The Moon gives out no light of its own; it reflects sunlight.\n\n**Supplement:** the average orbital speed is the circumference of the orbit divided by the time for one orbit: v = 2πr ÷ T.\n\n### The Solar System — 6.1.2\n\nThe Solar System contains:\n\n- one star, the Sun;\n- eight planets, in order from the Sun: Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune (a memory aid: My Very Easy Method Just Speeds Up Naming);\n- minor planets that orbit the Sun, including dwarf planets such as Pluto and the asteroids in the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter;\n- moons that orbit planets;\n- smaller bodies, including comets (balls of ice and dust in very elongated orbits) and natural satellites.\n\nThe four inner planets are small and rocky; the four outer planets are large and gaseous. **Accretion model** of how the Solar System formed:\n\n- It began as an interstellar cloud of gas and dust containing many elements.\n- Gravity pulled the cloud together; as it collapsed, it began to rotate faster and flattened into a spinning accretion disc, with most of the mass at the centre forming the Sun.\n- Close to the hot young Sun, only rocky, metallic materials with high melting points could stay solid, so small rocky planets formed there.\n- Further out, where it was cold, ices and large amounts of gas (hydrogen and helium) were available and were not blown away, so large gaseous planets formed.\n\n**Gravity in the Solar System:**\n\n- The gravitational field strength at a planet's surface depends on the planet's mass (more mass, stronger field).\n- The field around a planet gets weaker as the distance from the planet increases.\n- The Sun contains most (over 99%) of the mass of the Solar System. Its gravitational attraction is the force that keeps the planets, and any object orbiting the Sun, in orbit.\n\nLight from the Sun takes about 8 minutes to reach the Earth; light travels at 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s.\n\n**Supplement:** planets, minor planets and comets move in elliptical (oval) orbits. The Sun is not at the centre of the ellipse but at one focus, off to one side; only when an orbit is nearly circular is the Sun close to its centre. Comets have very elongated orbits.\n\n**Supplement:** the strength of the Sun's gravitational field decreases with distance from the Sun, so planets further out have lower orbital speeds (and much longer orbital periods, because their orbits are also longer).\n\n**Supplement:** an object in an elliptical orbit travels fastest when it is closest to the Sun. As it moves inwards, it loses gravitational potential energy and gains the same amount of kinetic energy (energy is conserved), so it speeds up. Moving out again, kinetic energy is converted back to gravitational potential energy, and it slows down.\n\n**Supplement — interpreting planetary data:**\n\n| Planet | Distance from Sun (million km) | Orbital period | Density (g/cm³) | Average surface temperature (°C) | Surface g (N/kg) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Mercury | 58 | 88 days | 5.4 | 170 | 3.7 |\n| Venus | 108 | 225 days | 5.2 | 460 | 8.9 |\n| Earth | 150 | 365 days | 5.5 | 15 | 9.8 |\n| Mars | 228 | 687 days | 3.9 | −65 | 3.7 |\n| Jupiter | 778 | 11.9 years | 1.3 | −110 | 24.8 |\n| Saturn | 1430 | 29.5 years | 0.7 | −140 | 10.4 |\n| Uranus | 2870 | 84 years | 1.3 | −195 | 8.9 |\n| Neptune | 4500 | 165 years | 1.6 | −200 | 11.2 |\n\n(Values are approximate. For the gas giants, the surface values are at the top of the clouds.)\n\nPatterns to look for: orbital period increases with distance; the inner rocky planets are dense and the outer gas giants have low densities (Saturn would float on water); temperature generally falls with distance, but Venus is hotter than Mercury because its thick carbon dioxide atmosphere traps energy; surface g depends on the planet's mass (and size), so massive Jupiter has the strongest field.\n\n### The Sun as a star — 6.2.1\n\nThe Sun is a medium-sized star, made mostly of hydrogen and helium. It radiates most of its energy as infrared, visible light and ultraviolet.\n\n**Supplement:** stars are powered by nuclear reactions that release energy. In stable stars, hydrogen nuclei fuse to form helium.\n\n### Stars and galaxies — 6.2.2\n\n- A galaxy is made up of many billions of stars.\n- The Sun is a star in the galaxy called the Milky Way.\n- The other stars in the Milky Way are much further from the Earth than the Sun is.\n- Astronomical distances are measured in light-years. A light-year is the distance travelled by light in (the vacuum of) space in one year. It is a distance, not a time.\n\n**Supplement:** one light-year is 9.5 × 10¹⁵ m.\n\n**Supplement — the life cycle of a star:**\n\n- A star forms from an interstellar cloud of gas and dust containing hydrogen (a nebula).\n- Gravity pulls the cloud together; it collapses and heats up, becoming a protostar.\n- When the centre is hot enough, hydrogen fuses into helium. The protostar becomes a stable star when the inward force of gravity is balanced by the outward force due to the high temperature (pressure) in its centre. The Sun is at this stage.\n- Eventually every star runs out of hydrogen in its core.\n- A star of about the Sun's mass expands to form a red giant. Its outer layers drift away as a planetary nebula, leaving a hot, small, dense white dwarf at the centre, which cools over a very long time.\n- A much more massive star expands to form a red supergiant, which then explodes as a supernova. This forms a nebula containing hydrogen and new heavier elements, and leaves behind a neutron star or, for the most massive stars, a black hole.\n- The nebula from a supernova may form new stars, with orbiting planets. The Sun and Earth contain heavy elements made in earlier stars.\n\n### The Universe — 6.2.3\n\n- The Milky Way is one of many billions of galaxies in the Universe. Its diameter is about 100 000 light-years.\n- **Redshift:** an increase in the observed wavelength of electromagnetic radiation from a star or galaxy that is moving away (receding) from us. The light shifts towards the red end of the spectrum.\n- Light from distant galaxies is redshifted compared with light from the same elements on Earth. The further away the galaxy, the greater the redshift, so the faster it is moving away.\n- This shows that the Universe is expanding, which supports the Big Bang Theory: the Universe began from a single point about 14 billion years ago and has been expanding ever since.\n\n**Supplement — cosmic microwave background radiation (CMBR):** microwave radiation of a specific frequency is detected coming from all directions in space. It was produced shortly after the Universe formed, as very high-energy (short-wavelength) radiation. As the Universe expanded, the radiation was stretched, so its wavelength increased into the microwave region. The CMBR is further evidence for the Big Bang.\n\n**Supplement — the Hubble constant:**\n\n- The speed v at which a galaxy moves away from us is found from the change in wavelength of its light (its redshift): a bigger change means a faster speed.\n- The distance d to a far galaxy is found from the brightness of a supernova in it: supernovae of a certain type have a known peak brightness, so the dimmer one appears, the further away it is.\n- Plotting v against d for many galaxies gives a straight line: v is proportional to d. The Hubble constant H₀ is the ratio v ÷ d. Its current estimate is 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ per second.\n- d ÷ v = 1 ÷ H₀ is the time a galaxy has taken to reach its present distance at its present speed. It is the same for every galaxy, so it estimates the age of the Universe, and suggests all the matter in the Universe was once at a single point.\n\n## Key formulas\n\n| Formula | Symbols and units | When to use it | Level |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| t = d ÷ v (light) | t: time, s; d: distance, m; v: speed of light, 3.0 × 10⁸ m/s | Time for light to cross the Solar System. Rearranged: d = vt. | Core |\n| 1 light-year = 9.5 × 10¹⁵ m | | Converting light-years to metres (× 9.5 × 10¹⁵) and back (÷). | Supplement |\n| v = 2πr ÷ T | v: average orbital speed, m/s; r: average orbital radius, m; T: orbital period, s | Planets, moons and satellites. Rearranged: T = 2πr ÷ v, r = vT ÷ 2π. | Supplement |\n| H₀ = v ÷ d | H₀: Hubble constant, 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ /s; v: recession speed, m/s; d: distance, m | Speed or distance of a far galaxy. Rearranged: v = H₀d, d = v ÷ H₀. | Supplement |\n| age of the Universe ≈ d ÷ v = 1 ÷ H₀ | s (divide by 3.15 × 10⁷ for years) | Estimating the age of the Universe. | Supplement |\n\nTime conversions: 1 day = 86 400 s; 1 year ≈ 3.15 × 10⁷ s.\n\n## Using the formulas in different situations\n\n### Situation: light travel time\n\n**Example — Sun to Earth.** Distance 1.5 × 10¹¹ m. t = 1.5 × 10¹¹ ÷ (3.0 × 10⁸) = 500 s, about 8.3 minutes. We see the Sun as it was 8 minutes ago.\n\n**Example — signal to Mars.** When Mars is 2.25 × 10¹¹ m away, a radio signal (also an EM wave) takes 2.25 × 10¹¹ ÷ (3.0 × 10⁸) = 750 s = 12.5 minutes. A reply takes another 12.5 minutes.\n\n**Example — Moon.** The Moon is 3.84 × 10⁸ m away. t = 3.84 × 10⁸ ÷ (3.0 × 10⁸) = 1.28 s.\n\n### Situation: light-years (Supplement)\n\n**Example — nearest star.** Proxima Centauri is 4.2 light-years away. d = 4.2 × 9.5 × 10¹⁵ = 4.0 × 10¹⁶ m. Its light takes 4.2 years to reach us.\n\n**Example — Milky Way.** 100 000 light-years = 1.0 × 10⁵ × 9.5 × 10¹⁵ = 9.5 × 10²⁰ m across.\n\n### Situation: orbital speed (Supplement)\n\n**Example — Earth.** r = 1.5 × 10¹¹ m, T = 365 days = 365 × 86 400 = 3.15 × 10⁷ s.\n\n- v = 2π × 1.5 × 10¹¹ ÷ (3.15 × 10⁷) = 3.0 × 10⁴ m/s (30 km/s).\n\n**Example — Moon.** r = 3.84 × 10⁸ m, T = 27.3 days = 2.36 × 10⁶ s. v = 2π × 3.84 × 10⁸ ÷ (2.36 × 10⁶) = 1.0 × 10³ m/s.\n\n**Example — find the period.** A satellite orbits at r = 7.0 × 10⁶ m with speed 7.5 × 10³ m/s. T = 2πr ÷ v = 2π × 7.0 × 10⁶ ÷ (7.5 × 10³) = 5900 s, about 98 minutes.\n\n**Example — compare planets.** Jupiter: r = 7.78 × 10¹¹ m, T = 11.9 years = 3.75 × 10⁸ s. v = 2π × 7.78 × 10¹¹ ÷ (3.75 × 10⁸) = 1.3 × 10⁴ m/s, less than half the Earth's speed: the further out, the slower.\n\n### Situation: Hubble's law (Supplement)\n\n**Example — find the speed.** A galaxy is 5.0 × 10²⁴ m away. v = H₀d = 2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸ × 5.0 × 10²⁴ = 1.1 × 10⁷ m/s.\n\n**Example — find the distance.** A galaxy recedes at 6.6 × 10⁶ m/s. d = v ÷ H₀ = 6.6 × 10⁶ ÷ (2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸) = 3.0 × 10²⁴ m. In light-years: 3.0 × 10²⁴ ÷ (9.5 × 10¹⁵) = 3.2 × 10⁸ light-years.\n\n**Example — age of the Universe.** 1 ÷ H₀ = 1 ÷ (2.2 × 10⁻¹⁸) = 4.5 × 10¹⁷ s. In years: 4.5 × 10¹⁷ ÷ (3.15 × 10⁷) = 1.4 × 10¹⁰ years, about 14 billion years.\n\n### Situation: reading planetary data (Supplement)\n\n**Example — which planet is gaseous?** From the table, Saturn's density (0.7 g/cm³) is far below the rocky planets' (about 4 to 5.5 g/cm³), so it is mostly gas.\n\n**Example — weight on another planet.** A 60 kg astronaut on Mars (g = 3.7 N/kg) weighs 60 × 3.7 = 222 N, compared with 588 N on Earth.\n\n## Exam checks\n\n- A light-year is a distance.\n- Day/night: rotation (24 h). Year and seasons: orbit (365 days) plus the tilted axis. Moon's phases: orbit (about a month).\n- Convert days or years into seconds before using v = 2πr ÷ T; r is the radius, not the diameter.\n- Inner planets: small, rocky, dense. Outer: large, gaseous. Explain with the accretion disc and temperature.\n- Faster when closer to the Sun in an elliptical orbit: gravitational potential energy changes into kinetic energy.\n- Star life cycle: nebula → protostar → stable star → red giant → planetary nebula + white dwarf (Sun-sized), or red supergiant → supernova → neutron star or black hole (massive).\n- Evidence for the Big Bang: redshift of distant galaxies (expanding Universe) and the CMBR.\n",
};
